window.CET6_BANKS.push({"id": "2017-06-1", "source": "2017年06月真题·第1套", "writing": {"prompt": "Directions: Suppose you are asked to give advice on whether to major in science or humanities at college, write an essay to state your opinion. You are required to write at least 150 words but no more than 200 words.", "sample": "When friends ask me whether to major in science or humanities, my advice is clear: let your genuine interest\nand personal strengths, rather than fashion or others' expectations, make the decision for you.\nI hold this view for two reasons. First, sustained interest is the true engine of academic success. A student who\nloves literature will happily read late into the night, while one forced into physics may soon burn out; passion,\nnot the label of a \"practical\" subject, keeps us going. Second, matching a major to one's natural aptitude greatly\nimproves the odds of excelling. Someone gifted at logic and calculation will thrive in engineering, whereas a\nperson skilled in language and empathy may shine in law or education. Choosing against your strengths only\nwastes precious years.\nTherefore, I would suggest that no one blindly follow the crowd toward whatever seems more profitable.\nInstead, look honestly inward, weigh what you enjoy and what you do well, and let that guide your choice. The\nright major is the one that fits you."}, "translation": {"prompt": "明朝统治中国276年，被人们描绘成人类历史上治理有序、社会稳定的最伟大的时代之一。这一时期，手工业的发展促进了市场经济和城市化。大量商品，包括酒和丝绸，都在市场销售。同时，还进口许多外国商品，如时钟和烟草。北京、南京、扬州、苏州这样的大商业中心相继形成。也是在明代，由郑和率领的船队曾到印度洋进行了七次大规模探险航行。还值得一提的是，中国文学的四大经典名著中有三部写于明代。", "sample": "The Ming Dynasty, which ruled China for 276 years, was described as one of the greatest, most well-governed and socially stable eras in human history. During this period, the development of handicrafts promoted the market economy and urbanization. A large number of goods, including wine and silk, were sold on the market. At the same time, many foreign goods, such as clocks and tobacco, were also imported. Large commercial centers such as Beijing, Nanjing, Yangzhou and Suzhou emerged one after another. It was also during the Ming Dynasty that the fleet led by Zheng He made seven large- scale voyages of exploration to the Indian Ocean. It is also worth mentioning that three of the four great classic novels of Chinese literature were written during the Ming Dynasty."}, "listeningUrl": "https://english-exam.lazynote.cn/cet6/paper/2017-06-1/?f=w", "clozePassage": "After becoming president of Purdue University in 2013, Mitch Daniels asked the faculty to prove that their students have actually achieved one of higher education's most important goals: critical thinking skills. Two years before, a nationwide study of college graduates had shown that more than a third had made no  __26__  gains in such mental abilities during their school years. Mr. Daniels needed to  __27__  the high cost of attending Purdue to its students and their families. After all, the percentage of Americans who say a college degree is \"very important\" has fallen  __28__  in the last 5-6 years.\nPurdue now has a pilot test to assess students' critical thinking skills. Yet like many college teachers around the U.S., the faculty remain  __29__  that their work as educators can be measured by a \"learning  __30__  \" such as a graduate's ability to investigate and reason. However, the professors need not worry so much. The results of a recent experiment showed that professors can use  __31__  metrics to measure how well students do in three key areas: critical thinking, written communication, and quantitative literacy.\nDespite the success of the experiment, the actual results are worrisome, and mostly  __32__  earlier studies. The organizers of the experiment concluded that far fewer students were achieving at high levels on critical thinking than they were doing for written communication or quantitative literacy. And that conclusion is based only on students nearing graduation.\nAmerican universities, despite their global  __33__  for excellence in teaching, have only begun to demonstrate what they can produce in real-world learning. Knowledge-based degrees are still important, but employers are  __34__  advanced thinking skills from college graduates. If the intellectual worth of a college degree can be  __35__  measured, more people will seek higher education—and come out better thinkers.", "matchIntro": "The Price of Oil and the Price of Carbon", "matchParagraphs": [{"label": "A", "text": "Fossil fuel prices are likely to stay \"low for long\". Notwithstanding important recent progress in developing renewable fuel sources, low fossil fuel prices could discourage further innovation in, and adoption of, cleaner energy technologies. The result would be higher emissions of carbon dioxide and other greenhouse gases."}, {"label": "B", "text": "Policymakers should not allow low energy prices to derail the clean energy transition. Action to restore appropriate price incentives, notably through corrective carbon pricing, is urgently needed to lower the risk of irreversible and potentially devastating effects of climate change. That approach also offers fiscal benefits."}, {"label": "C", "text": "Oil prices have dropped by over 60% since June 2014. A commonly held view in the oil industry is that \"the best cure for low oil prices is low oil prices\". The reasoning behind this saying is that low oil prices discourage investment in new production capacity, eventually shifting the oil supply curve backward and bringing prices back up as existing oil fields—which can be tapped at relatively low marginal cost—are depleted. In fact, in line with past experience, capital expenditure in the oil sector has dropped sharply in many producing countries, including the United States. The dynamic adjustment to low oil prices may, however, be different this time around."}, {"label": "D", "text": "Oil prices are expected to remain lower for longer. The advent of new technologies has added about 4.2 million barrels per day to the crude oil market, contributing to a global over-supply. In addition, other factors are putting downward pressure on oil prices: change in the strategic behavior of the Organization of Petroleum Exporting Countries, the projected increase in Iranian exports, the scaling-down of global demand (especially from emerging markets), the long-term drop in petroleum consumption in the United States, and some displacement of oil by substitutes. These likely persistent forces, like the growth of shale(页岩) oil, point to a \"low for long\" scenario. Futures markets, which show only a modest recovery of prices to around $60 a barrel by 2019, support this view."}, {"label": "E", "text": "Natural gas and coal—also fossil fuels—have similarly seen price declines that look to be long-lived. Coal and natural gas are mainly used for electricity generation, whereas oil is used mostly to power transportation, yet the prices of all these energy sources are linked. The North American shale gas boom has resulted in record low prices there. The recent discovery of the giant Zohr gas field off the Egyptian coast will eventually have impact on pricing in the Mediterranean region and Europe, and there is significant development potential in many other places, notably Argentina. Coal prices also are low, owing to over-supply and the scaling-down of demand, especially from China, which burns half of the world's coal."}, {"label": "F", "text": "Technological innovations have unleashed the power of renewables such as wind, hydro, solar, and geothermal(地热). Even Africa and the Middle East, home to economies that are heavily dependent on fossil fuel exports, have enormous potential to develop renewables. For example, the United Arab Emirates has endorsed an ambitious target to draw 24% of its primary energy consumption from renewable sources by 2021."}, {"label": "G", "text": "Progress in the development of renewables could be fragile, however, if fossil fuel prices remain low for long. Renewables account for only a small share of global primary energy consumption, which is still dominated by fossil fuels—30% each for coal and oil, 25% for natural gas. But renewable energy will have to displace fossil fuels to a much greater extent in the future to avoid unacceptable climate risks."}, {"label": "H", "text": "Unfortunately, the current low prices for oil, gas, and coal may provide little incentive for research to find even cheaper substitutes for those fuels. There is strong evidence that both innovation and adoption of cleaner technology are strongly encouraged by higher fossil fuel prices. The same is true for new technologies for alleviating fossil fuel emissions."}, {"label": "I", "text": "The current low fossil fuel price environment will thus certainly delay the energy transition from fossil fuel to clean energy sources. Unless renewables become cheap enough that substantial carbon deposits are left underground for a very long time, if not forever, the planet will likely be exposed to potentially catastrophic climate risks."}, {"label": "J", "text": "Some climate impacts may already be discernible. For example, the United Nations Children's Fund estimates that some 11 million children in Africa face hunger, disease, and water shortages as a result of the strongest El Nino(厄尔尼诺) weather phenomenon in decades. Many scientists believe that El Nino events, caused by warming in the Pacific, are becoming more intense as a result of climate change."}, {"label": "K", "text": "Nations from around the world have gathered in Paris for the United Nations Climate Change Conference, COP 21, with the goal of a universal and potentially legally-binding agreement on reducing greenhouse gas emissions. We need very broad participation to fully address the global tragedy that results when countries fail to take into account the negative impact of their carbon emissions on the rest of the world. Moreover, non-participation by nations, if sufficiently widespread, can undermine the political will of participating countries to act."}, {"label": "L", "text": "The nations participating at COP 21 are focusing on quantitative emissions-reduction commitments. Economic reasoning shows that the least expensive way for each country is to put a price on carbon emissions. The reason is that when carbon is priced, those emissions reductions that are least costly to implement will happen first. The International Monetary Fund calculates that countries can generate substantial fiscal revenues by eliminating fossil fuel subsidies and levying carbon charges that capture the domestic damage caused by emissions. A tax on upstream carbon sources is one easy way to put a price on carbon emissions, although some countries may wish to use other methods, such as emissions trading schemes. In order to maximize global welfare, every country's carbon pricing should reflect not only the purely domestic damage from emissions, but also the damage to foreign countries."}, {"label": "M", "text": "Setting the right carbon price will therefore efficiently align the costs paid by carbon users with the true social opportunity cost of using carbon. By raising relative demand for clean energy sources, a carbon price would also help align the market return to clean-energy innovation with its social return, spurring the refinement of existing technologies and the development of new ones. And it would raise the demand for technologies such as carbon capture and storage, spurring their further development. If not corrected by the appropriate carbon price, low fossil fuel prices are not accurately signaling to markets the true social profitability of clean energy. While alternative estimates of the damage from carbon emissions differ, and it's especially hard to reckon the likely costs of possible catastrophic climate events, most estimates suggest substantial negative effects."}, {"label": "N", "text": "Direct subsidies to research and development have been adopted by some governments but are a poor substitute for a carbon price: they do only part of the job, leaving in place market incentives to over-use fossil fuels and thereby add to the stock of atmospheric greenhouse gases without regard to the collateral(附带的) costs."}, {"label": "O", "text": "The hope is that the success of COP 21 opens the door to future international agreement on carbon prices. Agreement on an international carbon-price floor would be a good starting point in that process. Failure to address comprehensively the problem of greenhouse gas emissions, however, exposes all generations, present and future, to incalculable risks."}], "readingPassages": {"46": "Open data sharers are still in the minority in many fields. Although many researchers broadly agree that public access to raw data would accelerate science, most are reluctant to post the results of their own labors online.\nSome communities have agreed to share online—geneticists, for example, post DNA sequences at the GenBank repository(库), and astronomers are accustomed to accessing images of galaxies and stars from, say, the Sloan Digital Sky Survey, a telescope that has observed some 500 million objects—but these remain the exception, not the rule. Historically, scientists have objected to sharing for many reasons: it is a lot of work; until recently, good databases did not exist; grant funders were not pushing for sharing; it has been difficult to agree on standards for formatting data; and there is no agreed way to assign credit for data.\nBut the barriers are disappearing, in part because journals and funding agencies worldwide are encouraging scientists to make their data public. Last year, the Royal Society in London said in its report that scientists need to \"shift away from a research culture where data is viewed as a private preserve\". Funding agencies note that data paid for with public money should be public information, and the scientific community is recognizing that data can now be shared digitally in ways that were not possible before. To match the growing demand, services are springing up to make it easier to publish research products online and enable other researchers to discover and cite them.\nAlthough calls to share data often concentrate on the moral advantages of sharing, the practice is not purely altruistic(利他的). Researchers who share get plenty of personal benefits, including more connections with colleagues, improved visibility and increased citations. The most successful sharers—those whose data are downloaded and cited the most often—get noticed, and their work gets used. For example, one of the most popular data sets on multidisciplinary repository Dryad is about wood density around the world; it has been downloaded 5,700 times. Co-author Amy Zanne thinks that users probably range from climate-change researchers wanting to estimate how much carbon is stored in biomass, to foresters looking for information on different grades of timber. \"I'd much prefer to have my data used by the maximum number of people to ask their own questions,\" she says. \"It's important to allow readers and reviewers to see exactly how you arrive at your results. Publishing data and code allows your science to be reproducible.\"\nEven people whose data are less popular can benefit. By making the effort to organize and label files so others can understand them, scientists become more organized and better disciplined themselves, thus avoiding confusion later on.", "47": "Open data sharers are still in the minority in many fields. Although many researchers broadly agree that public access to raw data would accelerate science, most are reluctant to post the results of their own labors online.\nSome communities have agreed to share online—geneticists, for example, post DNA sequences at the GenBank repository(库), and astronomers are accustomed to accessing images of galaxies and stars from, say, the Sloan Digital Sky Survey, a telescope that has observed some 500 million objects—but these remain the exception, not the rule. Historically, scientists have objected to sharing for many reasons: it is a lot of work; until recently, good databases did not exist; grant funders were not pushing for sharing; it has been difficult to agree on standards for formatting data; and there is no agreed way to assign credit for data.\nBut the barriers are disappearing, in part because journals and funding agencies worldwide are encouraging scientists to make their data public. Last year, the Royal Society in London said in its report that scientists need to \"shift away from a research culture where data is viewed as a private preserve\". Funding agencies note that data paid for with public money should be public information, and the scientific community is recognizing that data can now be shared digitally in ways that were not possible before. To match the growing demand, services are springing up to make it easier to publish research products online and enable other researchers to discover and cite them.\nAlthough calls to share data often concentrate on the moral advantages of sharing, the practice is not purely altruistic(利他的). Researchers who share get plenty of personal benefits, including more connections with colleagues, improved visibility and increased citations. The most successful sharers—those whose data are downloaded and cited the most often—get noticed, and their work gets used. For example, one of the most popular data sets on multidisciplinary repository Dryad is about wood density around the world; it has been downloaded 5,700 times. Co-author Amy Zanne thinks that users probably range from climate-change researchers wanting to estimate how much carbon is stored in biomass, to foresters looking for information on different grades of timber. \"I'd much prefer to have my data used by the maximum number of people to ask their own questions,\" she says. \"It's important to allow readers and reviewers to see exactly how you arrive at your results. Publishing data and code allows your science to be reproducible.\"\nEven people whose data are less popular can benefit. By making the effort to organize and label files so others can understand them, scientists become more organized and better disciplined themselves, thus avoiding confusion later on.", "48": "Open data sharers are still in the minority in many fields. Although many researchers broadly agree that public access to raw data would accelerate science, most are reluctant to post the results of their own labors online.\nSome communities have agreed to share online—geneticists, for example, post DNA sequences at the GenBank repository(库), and astronomers are accustomed to accessing images of galaxies and stars from, say, the Sloan Digital Sky Survey, a telescope that has observed some 500 million objects—but these remain the exception, not the rule. Historically, scientists have objected to sharing for many reasons: it is a lot of work; until recently, good databases did not exist; grant funders were not pushing for sharing; it has been difficult to agree on standards for formatting data; and there is no agreed way to assign credit for data.\nBut the barriers are disappearing, in part because journals and funding agencies worldwide are encouraging scientists to make their data public. Last year, the Royal Society in London said in its report that scientists need to \"shift away from a research culture where data is viewed as a private preserve\". Funding agencies note that data paid for with public money should be public information, and the scientific community is recognizing that data can now be shared digitally in ways that were not possible before. To match the growing demand, services are springing up to make it easier to publish research products online and enable other researchers to discover and cite them.\nAlthough calls to share data often concentrate on the moral advantages of sharing, the practice is not purely altruistic(利他的). Researchers who share get plenty of personal benefits, including more connections with colleagues, improved visibility and increased citations. The most successful sharers—those whose data are downloaded and cited the most often—get noticed, and their work gets used. For example, one of the most popular data sets on multidisciplinary repository Dryad is about wood density around the world; it has been downloaded 5,700 times. Co-author Amy Zanne thinks that users probably range from climate-change researchers wanting to estimate how much carbon is stored in biomass, to foresters looking for information on different grades of timber. \"I'd much prefer to have my data used by the maximum number of people to ask their own questions,\" she says. \"It's important to allow readers and reviewers to see exactly how you arrive at your results. Publishing data and code allows your science to be reproducible.\"\nEven people whose data are less popular can benefit. By making the effort to organize and label files so others can understand them, scientists become more organized and better disciplined themselves, thus avoiding confusion later on.", "49": "Open data sharers are still in the minority in many fields. Although many researchers broadly agree that public access to raw data would accelerate science, most are reluctant to post the results of their own labors online.\nSome communities have agreed to share online—geneticists, for example, post DNA sequences at the GenBank repository(库), and astronomers are accustomed to accessing images of galaxies and stars from, say, the Sloan Digital Sky Survey, a telescope that has observed some 500 million objects—but these remain the exception, not the rule. Historically, scientists have objected to sharing for many reasons: it is a lot of work; until recently, good databases did not exist; grant funders were not pushing for sharing; it has been difficult to agree on standards for formatting data; and there is no agreed way to assign credit for data.\nBut the barriers are disappearing, in part because journals and funding agencies worldwide are encouraging scientists to make their data public. Last year, the Royal Society in London said in its report that scientists need to \"shift away from a research culture where data is viewed as a private preserve\". Funding agencies note that data paid for with public money should be public information, and the scientific community is recognizing that data can now be shared digitally in ways that were not possible before. To match the growing demand, services are springing up to make it easier to publish research products online and enable other researchers to discover and cite them.\nAlthough calls to share data often concentrate on the moral advantages of sharing, the practice is not purely altruistic(利他的). Researchers who share get plenty of personal benefits, including more connections with colleagues, improved visibility and increased citations. The most successful sharers—those whose data are downloaded and cited the most often—get noticed, and their work gets used. For example, one of the most popular data sets on multidisciplinary repository Dryad is about wood density around the world; it has been downloaded 5,700 times. Co-author Amy Zanne thinks that users probably range from climate-change researchers wanting to estimate how much carbon is stored in biomass, to foresters looking for information on different grades of timber. \"I'd much prefer to have my data used by the maximum number of people to ask their own questions,\" she says. \"It's important to allow readers and reviewers to see exactly how you arrive at your results. Publishing data and code allows your science to be reproducible.\"\nEven people whose data are less popular can benefit. By making the effort to organize and label files so others can understand them, scientists become more organized and better disciplined themselves, thus avoiding confusion later on.", "50": "Open data sharers are still in the minority in many fields. Although many researchers broadly agree that public access to raw data would accelerate science, most are reluctant to post the results of their own labors online.\nSome communities have agreed to share online—geneticists, for example, post DNA sequences at the GenBank repository(库), and astronomers are accustomed to accessing images of galaxies and stars from, say, the Sloan Digital Sky Survey, a telescope that has observed some 500 million objects—but these remain the exception, not the rule. Historically, scientists have objected to sharing for many reasons: it is a lot of work; until recently, good databases did not exist; grant funders were not pushing for sharing; it has been difficult to agree on standards for formatting data; and there is no agreed way to assign credit for data.\nBut the barriers are disappearing, in part because journals and funding agencies worldwide are encouraging scientists to make their data public. Last year, the Royal Society in London said in its report that scientists need to \"shift away from a research culture where data is viewed as a private preserve\". Funding agencies note that data paid for with public money should be public information, and the scientific community is recognizing that data can now be shared digitally in ways that were not possible before. To match the growing demand, services are springing up to make it easier to publish research products online and enable other researchers to discover and cite them.\nAlthough calls to share data often concentrate on the moral advantages of sharing, the practice is not purely altruistic(利他的). Researchers who share get plenty of personal benefits, including more connections with colleagues, improved visibility and increased citations. The most successful sharers—those whose data are downloaded and cited the most often—get noticed, and their work gets used. For example, one of the most popular data sets on multidisciplinary repository Dryad is about wood density around the world; it has been downloaded 5,700 times. Co-author Amy Zanne thinks that users probably range from climate-change researchers wanting to estimate how much carbon is stored in biomass, to foresters looking for information on different grades of timber. \"I'd much prefer to have my data used by the maximum number of people to ask their own questions,\" she says. \"It's important to allow readers and reviewers to see exactly how you arrive at your results. Publishing data and code allows your science to be reproducible.\"\nEven people whose data are less popular can benefit. By making the effort to organize and label files so others can understand them, scientists become more organized and better disciplined themselves, thus avoiding confusion later on.", "51": "Macy's reported its sales plunged 5.2% in November and December at stores open more than a year, a disappointing holiday season performance that capped a difficult year for a department store chain facing wide-ranging challenges. Its flagship stores in major U.S. cities depend heavily on international tourist spending, which shrank at many retailers due to a strong dollar. Meanwhile, Macy's has simply struggled to lure consumers who are more interested in spending on travel or dining out than on new clothes or accessories.\nThe company blamed much of the poor performance in November and December on unseasonably warm weather. \"About 80% of our company's year-over-year declines in comparable sales can be attributed to shortfalls(短缺) in cold-weather goods,\" said chief executive Terry Lundgren in a press release. This prompted the company to cut its forecasts for the full fourth quarter.\nHowever, it's clear that Macy's believes its troubles run deeper than a temporary aberration(偏离) off the thermometer. The retail giant said the poor financial performance this year has pushed it to begin implementing $400 million in cost-cutting measures. The company pledged to cut 600 back-office positions, though some 150 workers in those roles would be reassigned to other jobs. It also plans to offer \"voluntary separation\" packages to 165 senior executives. It will slash staffing at its fleet of 770 stores, a move affecting some 3,000 employees.\nThe retailer also announced the locations of 36 stores it will close in early 2016. The company had previously announced the planned closures, but had not said which locations would be affected. None of the chain's stores in the Washington metropolitan area are to be closed.\nMacy's has been moving aggressively to try to remake itself for a new era of shopping. It has plans to open more locations of Macy's Backstage, a newly-developed off-price concept which might help it better compete with ambitious T. J. Maxx. It's also pushing ahead in 2016 with an expansion of Bluemercury, the beauty chain it bought last year. At a time when young beauty shoppers are often turning to Sephora or Ulta instead of department store beauty counters, Macy's hopes Bluemercury will help strengthen its position in the category.\nOne relative bright spot for Macy's during the holiday season was the online channel, where it rang up \"double-digit\" increases in sales and a 25% increase in the number of orders it filled. That relative strength would be consistent with what was seen in the wider retail industry during the early part of the holiday season. While Thanksgiving, Black Friday and Cyber Monday all saw record spending online, in-store sales plunged over the holiday weekend.", "52": "Macy's reported its sales plunged 5.2% in November and December at stores open more than a year, a disappointing holiday season performance that capped a difficult year for a department store chain facing wide-ranging challenges. Its flagship stores in major U.S. cities depend heavily on international tourist spending, which shrank at many retailers due to a strong dollar. Meanwhile, Macy's has simply struggled to lure consumers who are more interested in spending on travel or dining out than on new clothes or accessories.\nThe company blamed much of the poor performance in November and December on unseasonably warm weather. \"About 80% of our company's year-over-year declines in comparable sales can be attributed to shortfalls(短缺) in cold-weather goods,\" said chief executive Terry Lundgren in a press release. This prompted the company to cut its forecasts for the full fourth quarter.\nHowever, it's clear that Macy's believes its troubles run deeper than a temporary aberration(偏离) off the thermometer. The retail giant said the poor financial performance this year has pushed it to begin implementing $400 million in cost-cutting measures. The company pledged to cut 600 back-office positions, though some 150 workers in those roles would be reassigned to other jobs. It also plans to offer \"voluntary separation\" packages to 165 senior executives. It will slash staffing at its fleet of 770 stores, a move affecting some 3,000 employees.\nThe retailer also announced the locations of 36 stores it will close in early 2016. The company had previously announced the planned closures, but had not said which locations would be affected. None of the chain's stores in the Washington metropolitan area are to be closed.\nMacy's has been moving aggressively to try to remake itself for a new era of shopping. It has plans to open more locations of Macy's Backstage, a newly-developed off-price concept which might help it better compete with ambitious T. J. Maxx. It's also pushing ahead in 2016 with an expansion of Bluemercury, the beauty chain it bought last year. At a time when young beauty shoppers are often turning to Sephora or Ulta instead of department store beauty counters, Macy's hopes Bluemercury will help strengthen its position in the category.\nOne relative bright spot for Macy's during the holiday season was the online channel, where it rang up \"double-digit\" increases in sales and a 25% increase in the number of orders it filled. That relative strength would be consistent with what was seen in the wider retail industry during the early part of the holiday season. While Thanksgiving, Black Friday and Cyber Monday all saw record spending online, in-store sales plunged over the holiday weekend.", "53": "Macy's reported its sales plunged 5.2% in November and December at stores open more than a year, a disappointing holiday season performance that capped a difficult year for a department store chain facing wide-ranging challenges. Its flagship stores in major U.S. cities depend heavily on international tourist spending, which shrank at many retailers due to a strong dollar. Meanwhile, Macy's has simply struggled to lure consumers who are more interested in spending on travel or dining out than on new clothes or accessories.\nThe company blamed much of the poor performance in November and December on unseasonably warm weather. \"About 80% of our company's year-over-year declines in comparable sales can be attributed to shortfalls(短缺) in cold-weather goods,\" said chief executive Terry Lundgren in a press release. This prompted the company to cut its forecasts for the full fourth quarter.\nHowever, it's clear that Macy's believes its troubles run deeper than a temporary aberration(偏离) off the thermometer. The retail giant said the poor financial performance this year has pushed it to begin implementing $400 million in cost-cutting measures. The company pledged to cut 600 back-office positions, though some 150 workers in those roles would be reassigned to other jobs. It also plans to offer \"voluntary separation\" packages to 165 senior executives. It will slash staffing at its fleet of 770 stores, a move affecting some 3,000 employees.\nThe retailer also announced the locations of 36 stores it will close in early 2016. The company had previously announced the planned closures, but had not said which locations would be affected. None of the chain's stores in the Washington metropolitan area are to be closed.\nMacy's has been moving aggressively to try to remake itself for a new era of shopping. It has plans to open more locations of Macy's Backstage, a newly-developed off-price concept which might help it better compete with ambitious T. J. Maxx. It's also pushing ahead in 2016 with an expansion of Bluemercury, the beauty chain it bought last year. At a time when young beauty shoppers are often turning to Sephora or Ulta instead of department store beauty counters, Macy's hopes Bluemercury will help strengthen its position in the category.\nOne relative bright spot for Macy's during the holiday season was the online channel, where it rang up \"double-digit\" increases in sales and a 25% increase in the number of orders it filled. That relative strength would be consistent with what was seen in the wider retail industry during the early part of the holiday season. While Thanksgiving, Black Friday and Cyber Monday all saw record spending online, in-store sales plunged over the holiday weekend.", "54": "Macy's reported its sales plunged 5.2% in November and December at stores open more than a year, a disappointing holiday season performance that capped a difficult year for a department store chain facing wide-ranging challenges. Its flagship stores in major U.S. cities depend heavily on international tourist spending, which shrank at many retailers due to a strong dollar. Meanwhile, Macy's has simply struggled to lure consumers who are more interested in spending on travel or dining out than on new clothes or accessories.\nThe company blamed much of the poor performance in November and December on unseasonably warm weather. \"About 80% of our company's year-over-year declines in comparable sales can be attributed to shortfalls(短缺) in cold-weather goods,\" said chief executive Terry Lundgren in a press release. This prompted the company to cut its forecasts for the full fourth quarter.\nHowever, it's clear that Macy's believes its troubles run deeper than a temporary aberration(偏离) off the thermometer. The retail giant said the poor financial performance this year has pushed it to begin implementing $400 million in cost-cutting measures. The company pledged to cut 600 back-office positions, though some 150 workers in those roles would be reassigned to other jobs. It also plans to offer \"voluntary separation\" packages to 165 senior executives. It will slash staffing at its fleet of 770 stores, a move affecting some 3,000 employees.\nThe retailer also announced the locations of 36 stores it will close in early 2016. The company had previously announced the planned closures, but had not said which locations would be affected. None of the chain's stores in the Washington metropolitan area are to be closed.\nMacy's has been moving aggressively to try to remake itself for a new era of shopping. It has plans to open more locations of Macy's Backstage, a newly-developed off-price concept which might help it better compete with ambitious T. J. Maxx. It's also pushing ahead in 2016 with an expansion of Bluemercury, the beauty chain it bought last year. At a time when young beauty shoppers are often turning to Sephora or Ulta instead of department store beauty counters, Macy's hopes Bluemercury will help strengthen its position in the category.\nOne relative bright spot for Macy's during the holiday season was the online channel, where it rang up \"double-digit\" increases in sales and a 25% increase in the number of orders it filled. That relative strength would be consistent with what was seen in the wider retail industry during the early part of the holiday season. While Thanksgiving, Black Friday and Cyber Monday all saw record spending online, in-store sales plunged over the holiday weekend.", "55": "Macy's reported its sales plunged 5.2% in November and December at stores open more than a year, a disappointing holiday season performance that capped a difficult year for a department store chain facing wide-ranging challenges. Its flagship stores in major U.S. cities depend heavily on international tourist spending, which shrank at many retailers due to a strong dollar. Meanwhile, Macy's has simply struggled to lure consumers who are more interested in spending on travel or dining out than on new clothes or accessories.\nThe company blamed much of the poor performance in November and December on unseasonably warm weather. \"About 80% of our company's year-over-year declines in comparable sales can be attributed to shortfalls(短缺) in cold-weather goods,\" said chief executive Terry Lundgren in a press release. This prompted the company to cut its forecasts for the full fourth quarter.\nHowever, it's clear that Macy's believes its troubles run deeper than a temporary aberration(偏离) off the thermometer. The retail giant said the poor financial performance this year has pushed it to begin implementing $400 million in cost-cutting measures. The company pledged to cut 600 back-office positions, though some 150 workers in those roles would be reassigned to other jobs. It also plans to offer \"voluntary separation\" packages to 165 senior executives. It will slash staffing at its fleet of 770 stores, a move affecting some 3,000 employees.\nThe retailer also announced the locations of 36 stores it will close in early 2016. The company had previously announced the planned closures, but had not said which locations would be affected. None of the chain's stores in the Washington metropolitan area are to be closed.\nMacy's has been moving aggressively to try to remake itself for a new era of shopping. It has plans to open more locations of Macy's Backstage, a newly-developed off-price concept which might help it better compete with ambitious T. J. Maxx. It's also pushing ahead in 2016 with an expansion of Bluemercury, the beauty chain it bought last year. At a time when young beauty shoppers are often turning to Sephora or Ulta instead of department store beauty counters, Macy's hopes Bluemercury will help strengthen its position in the category.\nOne relative bright spot for Macy's during the holiday season was the online channel, where it rang up \"double-digit\" increases in sales and a 25% increase in the number of orders it filled. That relative strength would be consistent with what was seen in the wider retail industry during the early part of the holiday season. While Thanksgiving, Black Friday and Cyber Monday all saw record spending online, in-store sales plunged over the holiday weekend."}, "questions": [{"id": "2017-06-1-l-1", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 1, "stem": "What is the number one factor that made employees happy according to the survey?", "options": ["A) Doing enjoyable work.", "B) Having friendly colleagues.", "C) Earning a competitive salary.", "D) Working for supportive bosses."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题） Having friendly colleagues.\n【定位】The most important factor, for the majority of the people interviewed, was having friendly,\nsupportive colleagues.\n对受访者中的大多数人来说，最重要的因素是拥有友好、给予支持的同事。\n【信号】最高级定第一。排序信号词 The most important factor（最重要的因素）直接点出\"排第\n一\"，紧跟其后的 having friendly, supportive colleagues 就是答案。听到 the most\nimportant 这类最高级，就锁定它后面带出的那个对象。\n【替换】having friendly, supportive colleagues ↔ Having friendly colleagues.\n删修饰留同义\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\n录音说 The second most important factor was having work that is enjoyable，Doing\nenjoyable work. 确被提到，但它是\"第二重要\"，题目问\"第一\"，盗用 enjoyable work 这\n个原词来骗\"听到就选\"。\n借用原文：The second most important factor was having work that is enjoyable\nC) 与原文相反\n录音明说 The two least important factors were … earning a competitive salary，薪水\n是\"最不重要\"之一，选项 Earning a competitive salary. 却被摆来当\"第一\"，方向正好相\n反。\n借用原文：The two least important factors were having one's achievements recognized and rather\nsurprisingly, earning a competitive salary\nD) 张冠李戴\n排第一的是\"同事\"colleagues，Working for supportive bosses. 借用了原文 supportive\n这个词，却把对象从\"同事\"偷换成\"老板\"，是张冠李戴。\n借用原文：having friendly, supportive colleagues", "points": ["听力·长对话", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2017-06-1-l-2", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 2, "stem": "What is the percentage of the people surveyed who felt unhappy at work?", "options": ["A) 31%.", "B) 20%.", "C) 25%.", "D) 73%."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题） 20%.\n【定位】25% of the working people interviewed described themselves as very happy at work.\nHowever, 20% of employees described themselves as being unhappy.\n25% 的受访在职人员形容自己在工作中非常开心。然而，20% 的员工形容自己不开心。\n【信号】转折后接目标数据。转折词 However 把句子分成两半：前半 25% … very happy 说开心的\n人，后半 20% of employees … unhappy 才是\"不开心\"的人。题目问 unhappy，要取转\n折后的 20%。\n【替换】20% of employees described themselves as being unhappy ↔ 20%.\n数字直接对应\n【排除】A) 未提及 31% 在整段录音里根本没出现过，纯属凑数干扰，没有任何原文支撑。\nC) 原词陷阱\n录音 25% … described themselves as very happy at work，25% 说的是\"很开心\"的人，\n25%. 盗用了这个真被念到的数字，却答非所问——题目问的是\"不开心\"的人。\n借用原文：25% of the working people interviewed described themselves as very happy at work\nD) 原词陷阱\n73% 来自第2段 73% people interviewed put their relationship with colleagues as the\nkey factor，说的是把同事关系当首要因素的人，与\"不开心\"无关，是远处搬来的原词陷\n阱。\n借用原文：73% people interviewed put their relationship with colleagues as the key factor", "points": ["听力·长对话", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2017-06-1-l-3", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 3, "stem": "What kind of companies are popular with employees?", "options": ["A) Those of a small size.", "B) Those run by women.", "C) Those that are well managed.", "D) Those full of skilled workers."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题） Those of a small size.\n【定位】First of all, small is beautiful. People definitely prefer working for smaller organizations or\ncompanies with less than 100 staff.\n首先，小即是美。人们明显更偏爱为规模较小的机构、或员工少于 100 人的公司工作。\n【信号】偏好词prefer点题。偏好信号 People definitely prefer working for smaller\norganizations 里的 prefer（更偏爱）直接点出受欢迎的类型是\"较小的公司\"，加重词\ndefinitely 进一步强调。听到 prefer 就锁定其后的特征。\n【替换】smaller organizations or companies with less than 100 staff ↔ Those of a small size.\n具体数概括为小\n【排除】B) 原词陷阱\n录音确有 women were happier in their work than men，但说的是\"女性比男性更开\n心\"，并非\"女性经营的公司受欢迎\"。Those run by women. 盗用 women 这个词，偷换成\n了\"公司由女性经营\"。\n借用原文：women were happier in their work than men\nC) 未提及\n\"管理得好\"听起来天然正确，但全段并未说员工偏爱管理好的公司，Those that are well\nmanaged. 是凭常识想当然、原文无据。\nD) 未提及\n录音从未提到\"员工技能高的公司\"，Those full of skilled workers. 无任何原文支撑，属凭\n空编造。", "points": ["听力·长对话", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2017-06-1-l-4", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 4, "stem": "What is the possible reason for people on part-time contracts to be happier?", "options": ["A) They can hop from job to job easily.", "B) They can win recognition of their work.", "C) They can better balance work and life.", "D) They can take on more than one job."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题） They can better balance work and life.\n【定位】And workers on part-time contracts who only work 4 or 5 hours a day are happier than those\nwho work full time. The researchers concluded that this is probably due to a better work-\nlife balance.\n而每天只工作 4 到 5 小时的兼职合同工，比全职工作的人更开心。研究者得出结论，这很可\n能是由于他们有更好的工作与生活平衡。\n【信号】因果due to给原因。因果信号 this is probably due to a better work-life balance 里的\ndue to（由于）直接点出原因。题干的 possible reason 正对应原文的 probably due to，\n听到 due to 就锁定其后的内容为答案。\n【替换】a better work-life balance ↔ They can better balance work and life.\n名词化转动词句\n【排除】A) 未提及\n录音从没说兼职者\"换工作容易\"，They can hop from job to job easily. 是凭\"兼职=灵\n活\"的常识想象出来的，原文无据。\nB) 原词陷阱\n\"获得认可\"对应的是第2段 having one's achievements recognized，且那里说它是最不重\n要的因素之一，与\"兼职者为何更开心\"毫不相干，是远处搬来的原词陷阱。\n借用原文：The two least important factors were having one's achievements recognized\nD) 过度推断\n录音说的是兼职者每天 only work 4 or 5 hours a day（工时短），从未说他们\"兼好几份\n工\"。They can take on more than one job. 把\"工时少\"曲解成\"工作多\"，方向相反且原文\n无此意。\n借用原文：workers on part-time contracts who only work 4 or 5 hours a day", "points": ["听力·长对话", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2017-06-1-l-5", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 5, "stem": "What does the man say about the book Moments Before the Flood?", "options": ["A) It is a book of European history.", "B) It is an introduction to music.", "C) It is about the city of Bruges.", "D) It is a collection of photos."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题） It is a collection of photos.\n【定位】This fear of a looming danger became the subject of a large-scale photo project.\n这种对迫近危险的恐惧，成了一个大型摄影项目的主题。\n【信号】受访者给作品定性的句子。对话题答案在被问者的应答里。男士在介绍这本书来历时，用\nbecame the subject of a large-scale photo project 给作品做了归类——\"摄影项目\"这个\n定性就是答案信号，它直接回答了\"这是一本什么书\"。\n【替换】photo project ↔ a collection of photos\n\"摄影项目\"=\"照片集\"，同义概括\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\n原文确实多次提到欧洲历史 rich in history，但那是说海岸\"历史悠久\"，并非说这本书是历\n史书；选项 a book of European history 拿\"history\"这个真听到的词来诱选，答非所问。\n借用原文：rich in history\nB) 原词陷阱\n原文出现 the concert hall of the city of Bruges(音乐厅)、concert season，选项 an\nintroduction to music 借\"concert/音乐\"这层听到的字眼诱你以为书与音乐有关，但音乐\n厅只是他接活拍照的地点，书本身与音乐无关。\n借用原文：the concert hall of the city of Bruges\nC) 原词陷阱\n原文出现 the city of Bruges，选项 about the city of Bruges 直接搬这个地名诱选，但\nBruges 只是邀请他拍照的城市，书的内容是海岸/海洋，并非\"写布鲁日这座城\"。\n借用原文：the city of Bruges", "points": ["听力·长对话", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2017-06-1-l-6", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 6, "stem": "When did the man get his idea for the work?", "options": ["A) When painting the concert hall of Bruges.", "B) When vacationing in an Italian coastal city.", "C) When taking pictures for a concert catalogue.", "D) When writing about Belgium's coastal regions."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题） When taking pictures for a concert catalogue.\n【定位】In 2006, when the concert hall of the city of Bruges asked me to take some pictures for a\ncatalogue for a new concert season around the theme of water, I found myself working along\nthe Belgian coastline.\n2006年，布鲁日市的音乐厅请我为新一季以\"水\"为主题的音乐会目录拍一些照片，我于是来\n到比利时海岸沿线工作。\n【信号】时间状语 In 2006 + when…引出的事件。受访者用 In 2006, when the concert hall…\nasked me to take some pictures for a catalogue 这个明确的时间+事件状语，交代了想\n法萌芽的时刻——\"为音乐会目录拍照\"这件事，正是答案信号。\n【替换】take some pictures for a catalogue for a new concert season ↔\ntaking pictures for a concert catalogue\n\"为音乐会季目录拍照\"=\"拍音乐会目录的照片\"，详述↔概括\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\n原文有 the concert hall of the city of Bruges，选项 When painting the concert hall of\nBruges. 把这个真听到的\"布鲁日音乐厅\"搬来，却把动作偷换成 painting(绘画)——他做的\n是拍照不是画画，动作错了。\n借用原文：the concert hall of the city of Bruges\nB) 未提及\n想法产生于2006年为音乐会目录拍照、沿比利时海岸工作时，原文从未说他在\"意大利海滨\n城市度假\"；选项 When vacationing in an Italian coastal city. 利用后文出现的意大利城市\nVenice 让人误以为想法起于那里，实则未提。\nD) 原词陷阱\n原文是 working along the Belgian coastline(沿比利时海岸拍照工作)，选项 When\nwriting about Belgium's coastal regions. 借\"比利时海岸\"这个真听到的地点，却把动作换\n成 writing(写作)——他是拍照不是写文章，且想法起于拍照那件事。\n借用原文：working along the Belgian coastline", "points": ["听力·长对话", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2017-06-1-l-7", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 7, "stem": "What will happen when the climate catastrophe occurs?", "options": ["A) The entire European coastline will be submerged.", "B) The rich heritage of Europe will be lost completely.", "C) The seawater of Europe will be seriously polluted.", "D) The major European scenic spots will disappear."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题） The entire European coastline will be submerged.\n【定位】It is clear now that it's a matter of time before the entire European coastline disappears\nunder water.\n现在已经很清楚，整条欧洲海岸线没入水下只是时间问题。\n【信号】断言式后果句 it's a matter of time before…。男士用 it's a matter of time before the\nentire European coastline disappears under water 这种\"早晚会发生\"的强断言，直接给\n出灾难后果——\"整条欧洲海岸线没入水下\"，这就是答案信号。\n【替换】the entire European coastline disappears under water ↔\nThe entire European coastline will be submerged.\n\"没入水下\"=\"被淹没\"，主被动+同义词替换\n【排除】B) 过度推断\n原文说海岸 rich in history(历史悠久)，选项 The rich heritage of Europe will be lost\ncompletely. 借\"rich/history\"这层听到的字眼，却把后果夸成\"欧洲的丰富遗产将彻底丧\n失\"——原文只说海岸线会被淹，没说\"遗产完全消失\"，completely 是过度推断。\n借用原文：rich in history\nC) 未提及\n原文讲的是海岸线被海水淹没，选项 The seawater of Europe will be seriously polluted.\n借\"sea/water\"的听感联想到\"海水污染\"，但原文从头到尾说的是\"淹没\"不是\"污染\"，属无中\n生有。\nD) 偷换概念\n原文是 the entire European coastline disappears under water(整条海岸线没入水下)，\n选项 The major European scenic spots will disappear. 借真听到的 disappear，却把范\n围从\"整条海岸线\"偷换成\"主要景点\"——范围被改小，主语对不上。\n借用原文：the entire European coastline disappears under water", "points": ["听力·长对话", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2017-06-1-l-8", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 8, "stem": "What does the man say about Venice?", "options": ["A) Its waterways are being increasingly polluted.", "B) People cannot get around without using boats.", "C) It attracts large numbers of tourists from home and abroad.", "D) Tourists use wooden paths to reach their hotels in the morning."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题） Tourists use wooden paths to reach their hotels in the morning.\n【定位】Also, Venice, the city eternally threatened by the sea, where every morning wooden pathways\nhave to be set up to allow tourists to reach their hotels.\n还有威尼斯，这座永远受海洋威胁的城市，每天早上都得架起木板路，好让游客走到他们的\n酒店。\n【信号】地名 Venice 后紧跟的描述句。受访者点出 Venice 后，紧接着 every morning wooden\npathways have to be set up to allow tourists to reach their hotels 这句具体描述，就是\n对威尼斯说法的答案信号——听到地名后第一句描述往往就是考点。\n【替换】every morning wooden pathways have to be set up to allow tourists to reach their hotels ↔\nTourists use wooden paths to reach their hotels in the morning.\n\"架木板路供游客到酒店\"=\"游客走木板路到酒店\"，被动↔主动改写\n【排除】A) 偷换概念\n原文说威尼斯 eternally threatened by the sea(永远受海洋威胁)，选项 Its waterways are\nbeing increasingly polluted. 借\"sea/water\"的听感把\"受海威胁\"偷换成\"水道日益被污\n染\"——威胁来自海水上涨/淹没，不是污染，概念被换。\n借用原文：eternally threatened by the sea\nB) 未提及\n原文讲威尼斯靠架木板路让游客到酒店，并没说\"人们离了船就出不了门\"；选项 People\ncannot get around without using boats. 利用大家对水城\"出行靠船\"的常识诱选，原文根\n本没提船。\nC) 原词陷阱\n原文出现 tourists(游客)，选项 It attracts large numbers of tourists from home and\nabroad. 借这个真听到的词编出\"吸引大量国内外游客\"，但原文只说游客需走木板路到酒\n店，从未说它\"吸引大量游客\"，答非所问。\n借用原文：tourists", "points": ["听力·长对话", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2017-06-1-l-9", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 9, "stem": "What do some people do when they face a new situation?", "options": ["A) They make careful preparation beforehand.", "B) They take too many irrelevant factors into account.", "C) They spend too much time anticipating their defeat.", "D) They try hard to avoid getting off on the wrong foot."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题） They spend too much time anticipating their defeat.\n【定位】When facing a new situation, some people tend to rehearse their defeat by spending too much\ntime anticipating the worst.\n面对新情境时，有些人往往会通过花太多时间预想最坏的结果来预演自己的失败。\n【信号】开头点题句。本句是篇章第一句，开门见山概括 some people 的做法，some people\ntend to rehearse their defeat 直接对应题干里的 some people，是典型的开头点题答\n案句。\n【替换】spending too much time anticipating the worst ↔ spend too much time anticipating their defeat\nthe worst 等于 defeat\nrehearse their defeat ↔ anticipating their defeat\n预演失败即预想失败\n【排除】A) 与原文相反\nmake careful preparation beforehand 说的是认真准备，与原文 rehearse their defeat\n（预演失败）方向相反；原文批评的正是这种消极心态，A 反而把它说成积极准备。\nB) 未提及\ntake too many irrelevant factors into account 说考虑太多无关因素，原文谈的是预想最\n坏结果，从没提 irrelevant factors，属凭空添加。\nD) 原词陷阱\ntry hard to avoid getting off on the wrong foot 借用了 avoid（避免）这类负面应对的\n语感，听着像在讲应对新情境，但原文 some people 的做法是预想失败，不是努力避免开\n局不顺，答非所问。\n借用原文：tend to rehearse their defeat", "points": ["听力·篇章", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2017-06-1-l-10", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 10, "stem": "What does the research conducted at Stanford University show?", "options": ["A) A person's nervous system is more complicated than imagined.", "B) Golfers usually have positive mental images of themselves.", "C) Mental images often interfere with athletes' performance.", "D) Thinking has the same effect on the nervous system as doing."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题） Thinking has the same effect on the nervous system as doing.\n【定位】Research conducted at Stanford University shows a mental image fires the nervous system\nthe same way as actually doing something.\n斯坦福大学开展的研究表明，一个心理意象对神经系统的激发方式，与真正去做某件事完全\n相同。\n【信号】research shows 引出结论。Research conducted at Stanford University shows 是明确\n的研究引出语，其后的 a mental image fires the nervous system the same way as\nactually doing something 即研究结论，正是答案句。\n【替换】a mental image fires the nervous system the same way as actually doing something ↔\nThinking has the same effect on the nervous system as doing\n意象激发即想的效果\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\nA person's nervous system is more complicated than imagined 盗用了原文反复出现的\nnervous system，但原文从没说神经系统更复杂，研究讲的是想与做效果相同，答非所\n问。\n借用原文：fires the nervous system the same way\nB) 张冠李戴\nGolfers usually have positive mental images of themselves 借用了原文 golfer 和\nmental image 两个原词，但原文的高尔夫例子讲的是球员想着别下水反而下水（负面），\n不是说球员有积极意象，张冠李戴。\n借用原文：when a golfer tells himself \"Don't hit the ball into the water,\" his mind sees the image\nof the ball flying into the water\nC) 过度推断\nMental images often interfere with athletes' performance 套用了 mental image，听\n着像在讲意象影响表现，但研究结论是想与做效果相同，并未说意象干扰运动员表现，超出\n原文。\n借用原文：a mental image fires the nervous system", "points": ["听力·篇章", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2017-06-1-l-11", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 11, "stem": "What advice does the speaker give to people in a stressful situation?", "options": ["A) Anticipate possible problems.", "B) Make a list of do's and don'ts.", "C) Picture themselves succeeding.", "D) Try to appear more professional."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题） Picture themselves succeeding.\n【定位】Consequently, before going into any stressful situation, focus only on what you want to\nhave happen.\n因此，在进入任何有压力的情境之前，只专注于你希望发生的事。\n【信号】建议句 focus only on。focus only on what you want to have happen 是祈使式的劝告\n句，前面还有 Consequently 收束全段、before going into any stressful situation 正对\n应题干 stressful situation，是明确的建议答案句。\n【替换】focus only on what you want to have happen ↔ Picture themselves succeeding\n想要发生的即成功画面\n【排除】A) 与原文相反\nAnticipate possible problems 是预想问题，恰是原文批评的 anticipating the worst 那种\n负面做法；作者建议的是专注想要发生的事，A 取了被否定的一面。\n借用原文：spending too much time anticipating the worst\nB) 原词陷阱\nMake a list of do's and don'ts 借用了原文 the \"don'ts\" syndrome 里的 don'ts，但原文\n把 don'ts 当作要避免的负面目标设定来批评，并没建议去列 do 和 don't 清单，答非所\n问。\n借用原文：the \"don'ts\" syndrome, a form of negative goal setting\nD) 张冠李戴\nTry to appear more professional 借用了下一段律师说的 I want to look professional\nand self-assured 里的 professional，但那是律师的目标、不是作者给所有压力情境者的建\n议，张冠李戴。\n借用原文：I want to look professional and self-assured", "points": ["听力·篇章", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2017-06-1-l-12", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 12, "stem": "What do we learn about the lawyer in the court?", "options": ["A) She wore a designer dress.", "B) She won her first jury trial.", "C) She did not speak loud enough.", "D) She presented moving pictures."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题） She won her first jury trial.\n【定位】A few weeks after this positive dress rehearsal, the young lawyer did win.\n在这次积极的预演几周之后，这位年轻律师真的赢了。\n【信号】例子收尾结论句。the young lawyer did win 是整个律师例子的收尾，did 还加重了语气强\n调真的赢了，正是对律师结果的明确交代，是答案句。\n【替换】the young lawyer did win ↔ She won her first jury trial\ndid win 即赢了官司\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\nShe wore a designer dress 说她穿名牌裙，原文 positive dress rehearsal 里的 dress 是\ndress rehearsal（彩排、预演）的一部分，根本不指穿裙子，属凭原词误解的臆造。\n借用原文：this positive dress rehearsal\nC) 与原文相反\nShe did not speak loud enough 说她声音不够响，原文恰恰相反，她想象 projecting her\nvoice, so it could be heard from the judge's bench to the back door（声音洪亮到全场\n可闻），极性反了。\n借用原文：projecting her voice, so it could be heard from the judge's bench to the back door\nD) 原词陷阱\nShe presented moving pictures 借用了原文反复出现的 picture / image，但原文里\npicture 指脑中想象的画面，不是她在庭上展示动人图片，答非所问。\n借用原文：your mind responds to pictures", "points": ["听力·篇章", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2017-06-1-l-13", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 13, "stem": "What does the new study tell about adding fiber to the teen diet?", "options": ["A) Its long-term effects are yet to be proved.", "B) Its health benefits have been overestimated.", "C) It helps people to avoid developing breast cancer.", "D) It enables patients with diabetes to recover sooner."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题） It helps people to avoid developing breast cancer.\n【定位】Research now says adding fiber to the teen diet may help lower the risk of breast cancer.\n现在有研究表明，在青少年饮食中加入纤维或许有助于降低患乳腺癌的风险。\n【信号】结论标志 Research now says。答案句以 Research now says（研究表明）领起，这是篇\n章里抛出核心结论的信号词；一段科普开头出现『研究表明……』，紧跟的就是全篇要论证\n的主张，正是本题答案所在。\n【替换】lower the risk of breast cancer ↔ avoid developing breast cancer\n降低风险=帮助避免\nadding fiber to the teen diet may help ↔ It helps people\nmay help=helps\n【排除】A) 未提及\nA 说 long-term effects are yet to be proved（长期效果尚待证明），但录音从头到尾在\n肯定纤维的好处、并说研究已证实，从未说效果『未被证明』，属凭空捏造的反向说法。\nB) 与原文相反\nB 说健康益处被 overestimated（高估），而录音通篇强调纤维益处真实有据，态度恰好相\n反；它盗用了全篇的 fiber、health 主题词来制造『好像在评价纤维』的错觉。\n借用原文：the benefits of fiber\nD) 原词陷阱\nD 说让 patients with diabetes to recover sooner（糖尿病患者更快康复）。录音末尾确\n实念到 diabetes，但只说高纤饮食与『降低糖尿病风险』相关，绝没说让糖尿病人『更快康\n复』；它盗用 diabetes 这个原词，把主线乳腺癌偷换成糖尿病来骗『听到原词就选』。\n借用原文：a reduced risk of heart disease and diabetes", "points": ["听力·篇章", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2017-06-1-l-14", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 14, "stem": "What do we learn about the survey of the 44,000 women?", "options": ["A) It focused on their ways of life during young adulthood.", "B) It tracked their change in food preferences for 20 years.", "C) It focused on their difference from men in fiber intake.", "D) It tracked their eating habits since their adolescence."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题） It tracked their eating habits since their adolescence.\n【定位】They were surveyed about their diets during high school, and their eating habits were tracked\nfor two decades.\n她们的高中时期饮食情况被调查过，其饮食习惯被追踪了二十年。\n【信号】调查设计直述句。答案句紧跟 a study of 44,000 women 之后，直接交代『调查了她们\nhigh school 时期的饮食、并追踪二十年』，是描述调查做法的直述句；数字 44,000 一\n出，后面这句就是细节落点。\n【替换】surveyed about their diets during high school ↔\ntracked their eating habits since their adolescence\nhigh school=adolescence\ntracked for two decades ↔ tracked\n原词 tracked 复现\n【排除】A) 偷换概念\nA 说聚焦 young adulthood（成年初期）的生活方式。录音调查的是 high school（青春\n期）阶段，不是成年初期；它利用考生分不清 young adulthood 与 adolescence 来偷换\n时间点。\n借用原文：during high school\nB) 原词陷阱\nB 说追踪了她们 change in food preferences（口味偏好的变化）二十年。录音确实念到\ntwo decades，B 盗用这个时长制造熟悉感，但追踪的是 eating habits（饮食习惯）而非\n『口味偏好的改变』，对象被偷换。\n借用原文：tracked for two decades\nC) 未提及\nC 说聚焦女性与男性在 fiber intake 上的差异。录音的调查对象只有 44,000 名女性，根本\n没拿男性来比较纤维摄入；这是凭空添加的男女对比。", "points": ["听力·篇章", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2017-06-1-l-15", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 15, "stem": "What explanation does the speaker offer for the research finding?", "options": ["A) Fiber may help to reduce hormones in the body.", "B) Fiber may bring more benefits to women than men.", "C) Fiber may improve the function of heart muscles.", "D) Fiber may make blood circulation more smooth."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题） Fiber may help to reduce hormones in the body.\n【定位】The finding points to long-standing evidence that fiber may reduce circulating female\nhormone levels, which could explain the reduced risk.\n这一发现指向一项长期存在的证据：纤维可能降低体内循环的女性激素水平，这或许能解释\n风险的降低。\n【信号】解释信号 explain。答案句里 which could explain the reduced risk 直接用 explain（解\n释）点明这是对结果的原因说明，前半句 fiber may reduce circulating female hormone\nlevels 就是给出的机制；explain 一词就是定位本题的信号。\n【替换】fiber may reduce circulating female hormone levels ↔\nFiber may help to reduce hormones in the body.\n降激素水平=降激素\n【排除】B) 原词陷阱\nB 说纤维 bring more benefits to women than men（对女性益处多于男性）。录音末尾\n确实提到 women…25 grams…men even more，B 盗用这处男女对比，但本题问『为什么\n能降风险』的机制，男女谁受益多答非所问。\n借用原文：women are told to eat 25 grams of fiber a day-men even more\nC) 偷换概念\nC 说纤维改善 heart muscles（心肌）功能。录音只说高纤饮食与『降低心脏病风险』相\n关，从没说改善心肌功能；它把 heart disease 的 heart 偷换成 heart muscles 的机制说\n法。\n借用原文：a reduced risk of heart disease and diabetes\nD) 原词陷阱\nD 说纤维让 blood circulation more smooth（血液循环更顺畅）。录音解释机制时只讲降\n低激素水平，circulating 是修饰 hormone 的『循环的激素』，并非说改善血液循环；D 利\n用 circulating 这个词造出录音没说的说法。\n借用原文：fiber may reduce circulating female hormone levels", "points": ["听力·篇章", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2017-06-1-l-16", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 16, "stem": "What is the speaker currently doing?", "options": ["A) Observing the changes in marketing.", "B) Conducting research on consumer behavior.", "C) Studying the hazards of young people drinking.", "D) Investigating the impact of media on government."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题） Conducting research on consumer behavior.\n【定位】Well, my current research is really about consumer behavior.\n嗯，我目前的研究其实是关于消费者行为的。\n【信号】开篇自报研究的点题句。录音一开口就用 my current research is really about consumer\nbehavior 点明当前研究主题，current 与题干 currently 直接对应，是说话人自报家门的点\n题句，独白体裁里这种开篇定调句最常承载第一题答案。\n【替换】my current research is really about consumer behavior ↔\nConducting research on consumer behavior.\n名词转动名词、意思不变\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\n录音第三段确有 Markets always change faster than marketing，A 项 Observing the\nchanges in marketing. 借用了 marketing/change 这些原词，但那是说话人解释消费者行\n为为何要研究时的一句论断，不是他当前在做的事，听到 marketing、change 就选就中了\n原词陷阱。\n借用原文：Markets always change faster than marketing\nC) 原词陷阱\nC 项 Studying the hazards of young people drinking. 借用了 young people's drinking\n这个原词，青年饮酒只是说话人在消费者行为大主题下举的一个研究例子，且录音并未把它\n定性为 hazards（危害），把它当成他当前研究的全部是以偏概全、答非所问。\n借用原文：young people's drinking\nD) 原词陷阱\nD 项 Investigating the impact of media on government. 拼用了录音里的 media 与\ngovernment 两个原词，但录音说的是 how older people are represented in the media\n（老年人在媒体中如何被呈现）以及青年饮酒是 a major concern to government，从未\n研究『媒体对政府的影响』，这是把两个原词强凑成一个根本没说过的因果关系。\n借用原文：how older people are represented in the media", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2017-06-1-l-17", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 17, "stem": "What has the speaker found about young people's drinking?", "options": ["A) It is the cause of many street riots.", "B) It is a chief concern of parents.", "C) It is getting worse year by year.", "D) It is an act of socialising."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题） It is an act of socialising.\n【定位】in the case of young people's drinking, one of the things that I've identified is that drinking for\npeople, say, between the ages of 18 and 24 is all about the social activity.\n就青年饮酒而言，我所发现的一点是，对于大约 18 到 24 岁的人来说，饮酒完全是关于社\n交活动的。\n【信号】I've identified 引出的发现判定句。录音用 one of the things that I've identified is that\ndrinking … is all about the social activity 给出明确发现，identified 与题干 found 同\n义、is all about 是下定性的强判定结构，正是这句把饮酒定性为社交，是答案落点。\n【替换】drinking for people, say, between the ages of 18 and 24 is all about the social activity ↔\nIt is an act of socialising.\nsocial activity 同义改写为 act of socialising\n【排除】A) 未提及\nA 项 It is the cause of many street riots. 是凭『青年喝酒』的刻板印象联想出来的负面后\n果，录音里既没出现 riots，也从未说饮酒导致骚乱，属于录音根本没提的内容。\nB) 张冠李戴\nB 项 It is a chief concern of parents. 借用了录音 a major concern to government 的\nconcern 一词，却把担忧的主体由 government（政府）偷换成 parents（家长），主体一\n换就成了张冠李戴。\n借用原文：a major concern to government\nC) 过度推断\nC 项 It is getting worse year by year. 是从『政府很担忧』过度推断出的恶化趋势，但录\n音只说它是 concern、是 social activity，从未说饮酒一年比一年严重，year by year 的趋\n势是录音没有的推断。", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2017-06-1-l-18", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 18, "stem": "What does the speaker say that his students did last year?", "options": ["A) They spent a week studying their own purchasing behavior.", "B) They researched the impact of mobile phones on young people.", "C) They analysed their family budgets over the years.", "D) They conducted a thorough research on advertising."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题） They spent a week studying their own purchasing behavior.\n【定位】last year my students spent a week looking at their own purchasing and analysed it in detail\nfrom shopping to the relationship that they have with their retail banks and their mobile\nphone providers.\n去年我的学生们花了一周时间审视他们自己的购买行为，并从购物到他们与零售银行、手机\n运营商的关系，进行了详细分析。\n【信号】last year 时间词直接对应题干。录音里 last year my students spent a week looking at\ntheir own purchasing 的 last year 与题干 last year 逐字对应，spent a week 又与某选项\n的 a week 呼应，是顺时间词即可锁定的答案句。\n【替换】spent a week looking at their own purchasing ↔\nThey spent a week studying their own purchasing behavior.\nlooking at 同义为 studying，purchasing 扩为 purchasing behavior\n【排除】B) 原词陷阱\nB 项 They researched the impact of mobile phones on young people. 借用了录音\nmobile phone providers 的 mobile phone 一词，但原文只是说学生分析自己购买行为时\n涉及与手机运营商的关系，并非研究『手机对青年的影响』，研究对象被偷换。\n借用原文：mobile phone providers\nC) 原词陷阱\nC 项 They analysed their family budgets over the years. 拼用了录音里的 budgets 与\nyears，但原文 helped them identify just what kind of budgets they had 说的是这次活\n动顺带帮学生看清自己的预算，并非『多年来分析家庭预算』，family、over the years 都\n是凑出来的。\n借用原文：helped them identify just what kind of budgets they had\nD) 原词陷阱\nD 项 They conducted a thorough research on advertising. 借用了录音 how\nconsumers are presented in advertising 的 advertising，但广告是说话人课堂教学讨论\n的内容，去年学生具体做的研究是审视自己的购买行为，把课堂内容当成学生去年所做的研\n究是张冠李戴。\n借用原文：how consumers are presented in advertising", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2017-06-1-l-19", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 19, "stem": "What do we learn about Sweden?", "options": ["A) It is helping its banks to improve efficiency.", "B) It is trying hard to do away with dirty money.", "C) It is the first country to use credit cards in the world.", "D) It is likely to give up paper money in the near future."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题） It is likely to give up paper money in the near future.\n【定位】Sweden was the first European country to print and use paper money, but it may soon do\naway with physical currencies.\n瑞典是欧洲第一个印制并使用纸币的国家，但它可能很快就要废弃实体货币了。\n【信号】转折 but 后才是真意。这句以 but 把语义拐了向：前半 the first European country to\nprint and use paper money 是背景铺垫，转折后的 it may soon do away with physical\ncurrencies 才是录音真正要点出的关于瑞典的现状，答案落在 but 之后。\n【替换】it may soon do away with physical currencies ↔\nIt is likely to give up paper money in the near future.\ndo away with = give up，soon = near future\n【排除】A) 未提及\n录音讲银行好处时提到省钱、避监管，但没说瑞典在帮银行 improve efficiency，\"提升效\n率\"是把\"省钱避麻烦\"过度引申，原文未提。\nB) 原词陷阱\n录音确实念到 dirty money，但那是说改用无现金系统能\"避免\"脏钱，并非说瑞典在\"努力\n清除\"脏钱，选项 trying hard to do away with dirty money 借这个原词制造\"听到就想\n选\"，实则答非所问。\n借用原文：they can also avoid bank robberies, theft, and dirty money\nC) 与原文相反\n录音说瑞典是 the first European country to print and use paper money（第一个用纸\n币），选项 the first country to use credit cards 保留了 the first 这个最高级框架，却把\npaper money 换成 credit cards，并把范围从 European 扩成 in the world，与原文不\n符。\n借用原文：Sweden was the first European country to print and use paper money", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2017-06-1-l-20", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 20, "stem": "What did Claer Barrett want to find out with her experiment?", "options": ["A) Whether it is possible to travel without carrying any physical currency.", "B) Whether it is possible to predict how much money one is going to spend.", "C) Whether the absence of physical currency causes a person to spend more.", "D) Whether the absence of physical currency is going to affect everyday life."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （目的题） Whether the absence of physical currency causes a person to spend more.\n【定位】Barrett wanted to find out if the absence of physical currency does indeed cause a person to\nspend more, so she decided to conduct an experiment a few months ago.\n巴雷特想弄清楚，没有实体货币是否真的会导致一个人花更多钱，于是几个月前她决定做一\n个实验。\n【信号】find out if 后即验证目标。录音用 wanted to find out if 直接挑明实验目的，紧跟其后的\nthe absence of physical currency does indeed cause a person to spend more 就是她\n要验证的命题，这是答案句的明确信号。\n【替换】the absence of physical currency does indeed cause a person to spend more ↔\nWhether the absence of physical currency causes a person to spend more.\n几乎同句，仅去掉 does indeed\n【排除】A) 答非所问\n选项 travel without carrying any physical currency 虽与火车那段\"很多人没带现金\"沾\n边，但那是实验过程中的插曲，并非她想\"验证\"的命题，答非所问。\n借用原文：many of the passengers were traveling without cash\nB) 原词陷阱\n录音确实念到 hard to predict how much cash one is going to need，但那是实验的\"结\n果/发现\"，不是她一开始\"想验证\"的命题；选项 predict how much money one is going\nto spend 借 predict 这个原词把发现偷换成目的。\n借用原文：it is incredibly hard to predict how much cash one is going to need\nD) 偷换概念\n录音里 less a feature of our everyday lives 出现过 everyday life，选项 affect everyday\nlife 借此把验证目标放大成\"是否影响日常生活\"，比她实际要验证的\"是否让人花更多\"范围\n大得多。\n借用原文：less a feature of our everyday lives", "points": ["听力·讲座", "目的题"]}, {"id": "2017-06-1-l-21", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 21, "stem": "What did Claer Barrett find on her train ride?", "options": ["A) There was no food service on the train.", "B) The service on the train was not good.", "C) The restaurant car accepted cash only.", "D) The cash in her handbag was missing."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题） The restaurant car accepted cash only.\n【定位】On the way, there was an announcement that the restaurant car was not currently accepting\ncredit cards. The train cars were filled with groans because many of the passengers were\ntraveling without cash.\n途中，广播通知说餐车目前不接受信用卡。车厢里一片抱怨声，因为许多乘客出行都没带\n现金。\n【信号】announcement 引出关键事件。there was an announcement that 引出火车上发生的关\n键事件 the restaurant car was not currently accepting credit cards，后一句 because\nmany of the passengers were traveling without cash 解释了为何引发抱怨，事件落点\n清晰。\n【替换】the restaurant car was not currently accepting credit cards ↔\nThe restaurant car accepted cash only.\n不收信用卡 = 只收现金\n【排除】A) 与原文相反\n录音说的是餐车 not currently accepting credit cards（只是不收卡），并非 no food\nservice（没有餐食服务）；餐车照常营业，只是付款方式受限，与\"没有餐食\"相反。\n借用原文：the restaurant car was not currently accepting credit cards\nB) 未提及\n录音只说餐车不收信用卡、乘客抱怨，没有评价 service on the train was not good（服\n务好不好）；抱怨是因为没带现金付不了，不是因为服务差。\nD) 张冠李戴\n录音第 3 段提到她 still finding cash stuffed in her trouser pockets and the pockets of\nher handbags，handbag 与 cash 出现过，但说的是\"手袋里还塞着现金\"，并非 cash in\nher handbag was missing（手袋里的现金不见了），更不在火车那段。\n借用原文：she was still finding cash stuffed in her trouser pockets and the pockets of her handbags", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2017-06-1-l-22", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 22, "stem": "How did people of the last generation budget their spending?", "options": ["A) By putting money into envelopes.", "B) By drawing money week by week.", "C) By limiting their day-to-day spending.", "D) By refusing to buy anything on credit."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题） By putting money into envelopes.\n【定位】My parents, when they were younger, used to budget by putting money into envelopes—\nthey'd get paid and they'd immediately separate the cash into piles and put them in envelopes,\nso they knew what they had to spend week by week.\n我父母年轻时常常这样做预算：把钱放进信封里——一发工资就立刻把现金分成几摞放进信\n封，这样他们就知道每周该花多少。\n【信号】budget by 后即方法动作。budget by putting money into envelopes 用 by 直接点出方\n法动作，是这道\"问方式\"题的答案信号；其后 put them in envelopes 进一步坐实\"装信\n封\"这一做法。\n【替换】budget by putting money into envelopes ↔ By putting money into envelopes.\n几乎原句保留\n【排除】B) 原词陷阱\n录音念到 they knew what they had to spend week by week，week by week 是\"按周心\n里有数\"的结果，并非 drawing money week by week（按周取钱）这个动作；选项借\nweek by week 这个原词制造\"听到就想选\"，实则把方法说错了。\n借用原文：so they knew what they had to spend week by week\nC) 过度推断\n装信封的结果是控制了花销，但录音的方法是\"放进信封\"，并非直接说 limiting their day-\nto-day spending（限制日常开支）这种笼统做法；这是把具体方法过度概括成一个泛泛说\n法。\nD) 原词陷阱\n录音说上一代用信封管钱，contrast 的是 nowadays, we're all on credit cards（如今我们\n都刷信用卡），但并没有说他们\"拒绝任何赊账\"；refusing to buy anything on credit 是\n借 credit 这个原词凭空安给上一代的做法。\n借用原文：we're all on credit cards", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2017-06-1-l-23", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 23, "stem": "What is one of the problems the speaker mentions in his talk?", "options": ["A) Population explosion.", "B) Chronic hunger.", "C) Extinction of rare species.", "D) Environmental deterioration."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题） Chronic hunger.\n【定位】Right now, a billion people are chronically hungry. That means they wake up hungry, they are\nhungry all day and they go to sleep hungry.\n现在，有十亿人长期处于饥饿之中。这意味着他们醒来时是饿的、整天都是饿的、睡觉时还\n是饿的。\n【信号】举例罗列问题的首项。讲者用 so let's start with a few problems 开启问题清单，紧接着 a\nbillion people are chronically hungry 就是罗列的第一个问题，这种以 start with / a few\nproblems 引出的并列举例，每一项都是答案候选，chronically hungry 正是其中一项。\n【替换】a billion people are chronically hungry ↔ Chronic hunger.\n形容词短语改名词短语\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\n全篇主题是人口（population），考生易把 Population explosion.（人口爆炸）当成必然\n提到的问题，但录音从未说人口爆炸，讲的是 aging of the population（人口老龄化）和\n具体困境，A 属听到 population 就想选的原词陷阱。\n借用原文：the aging of the population\nC) 原词陷阱\nExtinction of rare species.（珍稀物种灭绝）里 species 在第三段确实念到，但那是讲\ndemography 也研究非人类物种，并非把物种灭绝列为问题；C 盗用 species 一词张冠李\n戴。\n借用原文：the populations of non-human species\nD) 未提及\nEnvironmental deterioration.（环境恶化）是谈人口最容易联想到的问题，很合常识，但\n录音列举的问题是饥饿、贫民窟、文盲、意外怀孕，从未提环境恶化，属于凭常识脑补的未\n提及项。", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2017-06-1-l-24", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 24, "stem": "What does the speaker say about pregnancies?", "options": ["A) They contribute to overpopulation.", "B) About half of them are unintended.", "C) They have been brought under control.", "D) The majority of them tend to end halfway."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题） About half of them are unintended.\n【定位】This is not only a problem in developing countries; about half of all pregnancies globally\nare unintended.\n这不仅仅是发展中国家的问题；全球大约一半的怀孕都是意外（非计划内）的。\n【信号】锚词 pregnancies 直接出现。答案句直接念出题目锚词 about half of all pregnancies\nglobally are unintended，且带量化词 about half，正是预读时预备要记的比例数字，听\n到 pregnancies 配 half 即可锁定。\n【替换】about half of all pregnancies globally are unintended ↔ About half of them are unintended.\n近乎同形＋代词替换\n【排除】A) 过度推断\nThey contribute to overpopulation. 把意外怀孕推向导致人口过剩，是常识联想；录音只\n说一半怀孕是意外，从未说怀孕导致人口过剩，且 overpopulation 一词录音也没出现，属\n脑补的因果。\nC) 与原文相反\n录音明说 This is not only a problem in developing countries，意思是问题没被局限、没\n被解决；They have been brought under control.（已被控制）与之相反，录音传达的恰\n是问题仍普遍存在。\n借用原文：This is not only a problem in developing countries\nD) 偷换概念\nThe majority of them tend to end halfway. 把 half（一半）偷换成 majority（大多\n数），又把 unintended（意外）换成 end halfway（半途结束），数字和论断都被改，录\n音没有半途结束这一说。", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2017-06-1-l-25", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 25, "stem": "How does the speaker view the study of the populations?", "options": ["A) It is essential to the wellbeing of all species on earth.", "B) It is becoming a subject of interdisciplinary research.", "C) It is neglected in many of the developing countries.", "D) It is beginning to attract postgraduates' attention."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （态度观点题） It is essential to the wellbeing of all species on earth.\n【定位】It is the means to intervene more wisely and more effectively in the real world to improve the\nwell being not only of yourself, important as that may be, but of people around you and of\nother species with whom we share the planet.\n它（人口学）是更明智、更有效地干预现实世界的手段，用以增进福祉——不只是你自己\n的福祉（尽管那也很重要），还有你周围人的福祉，以及与我们共享这颗星球的其他物种的\n福祉。\n【信号】结尾总结定性句。讲者在末段以 I see demography as a central subject 起评，再用 to\nimprove the well being … of other species with whom we share the planet 收束，这\n种 I see … as / the means to 的总结定性正是态度题的答案落点。\n【替换】improve the well being not only of yourself … but of people around you and of other species with\nwhom we share the planet\n↔ It is essential to the wellbeing of all species on earth.\n列举范围概括为 all species\nI see demography as a central subject ↔ It is essential\ncentral 与 essential 同义定性\n【排除】B) 原词陷阱\nIt is becoming a subject of interdisciplinary research. 借录音里 demography is …\nrelated to economics 的跨学科气息编出 interdisciplinary research，但讲者只说它与经\n济学相关，从未说它正成为一门跨学科研究的对象，是原词联想的拼凑。\n借用原文：a central subject related to economics\nC) 张冠李戴\ndeveloping countries 在第二段确实念到（not only a problem in developing\ncountries），但那是讲意外怀孕，不是讲人口研究被忽视；neglected in many of the\ndeveloping countries 把该地点词挪来安到研究头上，张冠李戴。\n借用原文：not only a problem in developing countries\nD) 偷换概念\n录音开头讲 taking a course in demography in college，是劝大学生选修；It is\nbeginning to attract postgraduates' attention. 把 college 学生悄悄换成\npostgraduates（研究生），且讲者问的是看法而非谁在关注，属偷换加未提及。\n借用原文：taking a course in demography in college", "points": ["听力·讲座", "态度观点题"]}, {"id": "2017-06-1-c-26", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 26, "stem": "", "options": ["A) accurately", "B) confirm", "C) demanding", "D) doubtful", "E) drastically", "F) justify", "G) monopolized", "H) outcome", "I) predominance", "J) presuming", "K) reputation", "L) significant", "M) signify", "N) simultaneously", "O) standardized"], "answer": "L", "analysis": "【答案】L) significant\n形容词\n【词性槽】形容词修饰名词 gains\n【依据】搭配 significant gains 表\"显著进步\"\n句意是\"超过三分之一的人在心智能力上**没有显著进步**\"，significant gains 是英文里常\n见固定搭配，正好回扣前一句 asked the faculty to prove that their students have\nactually achieved one of higher education's most important goals: critical thinking\nskills 的\"是否真有提升\"议题。\n【竞争词】C) demanding 义不通：要求高的进步\ndemanding 意为\"要求高的、费力的\"，常修饰 job/task/customer，与 gains（进步、收益）\n搭不上。\nD) doubtful 义不通：可疑的进步\ndoubtful 意为\"怀疑的、没把握的\"，多修饰人的态度（doubtful about…）或前景（doubtful\nfuture），不与 gains 搭配。\nO) standardized 义不通：标准化的进步\nstandardized 一般搭配 test/metrics/procedure 表\"被统一规格化的\"，放在 gains 前不合常\n理，也与下文\"是否进步\"的语义无关。", "points": ["选词填空", "词性判断·形容词修饰名词 gains"]}, {"id": "2017-06-1-c-27", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 27, "stem": "", "options": ["A) accurately", "B) confirm", "C) demanding", "D) doubtful", "E) drastically", "F) justify", "G) monopolized", "H) outcome", "I) predominance", "J) presuming", "K) reputation", "L) significant", "M) signify", "N) simultaneously", "O) standardized"], "answer": "F", "analysis": "【答案】F) justify\n动词\n【词性槽】needed to 后跟动词原形\n【依据】向家庭\"证明高学费正当\"\n本段语境是 asked the faculty to prove that their students have actually achieved one\nof higher education's most important goals，校长要让学生与家庭觉得高学费物有所\n值，justify the high cost 意为\"为高学费提供正当性辩护\"，搭配地道、语义贴合。\n【竞争词】B) confirm 义偏：确认高学费\nconfirm 表\"证实某事真实存在\"，confirm the high cost 像是\"确认这学费确实贵\"，但事实无需\n证实，校长要做的是\"为之辩护\"，方向偏离。\nM) signify 义不通：表示高学费\nsignify 意为\"意味着、表示\"，主语通常是符号或现象，主语是\"Mr. Daniels\"加 signify the cost\n读不通，搭配不成立。", "points": ["选词填空", "词性判断·needed to 后跟动词原形"]}, {"id": "2017-06-1-c-28", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 28, "stem": "", "options": ["A) accurately", "B) confirm", "C) demanding", "D) doubtful", "E) drastically", "F) justify", "G) monopolized", "H) outcome", "I) predominance", "J) presuming", "K) reputation", "L) significant", "M) signify", "N) simultaneously", "O) standardized"], "answer": "E", "analysis": "【答案】E) drastically\n副词\n【词性槽】副词修饰动词 fallen\n【依据】急剧下降，承接上文\"重要性被怀疑\"\n上一句刚说大学校长要 justify the high cost of attending Purdue to its students and\ntheir families，本句给出原因——民众对大学价值的认可\"急剧下降\"。drastically 与 fall 搭\n配表\"骤降\"，正好为\"After all\"提供有力论据。\n【竞争词】A) accurately 义不通：准确地下降\naccurately 意为\"准确地\"，描写测量或表述的精度，不修饰\"下降\"的程度，逻辑上说不通。\nN) simultaneously 义不通：同时下降\nsimultaneously 强调\"同时发生\"，本句只谈了\"百分比下降\"一件事，没有第二件事来呼应\"同\n时\"，逻辑链不完整。", "points": ["选词填空", "词性判断·副词修饰动词 fallen"]}, {"id": "2017-06-1-c-29", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 29, "stem": "", "options": ["A) accurately", "B) confirm", "C) demanding", "D) doubtful", "E) drastically", "F) justify", "G) monopolized", "H) outcome", "I) predominance", "J) presuming", "K) reputation", "L) significant", "M) signify", "N) simultaneously", "O) standardized"], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D) doubtful\n形容词\n【词性槽】系动词 remain 后接形容词作表语\n【依据】教师对量化教学心存疑虑\ndoubtful that 是固定搭配，意为\"怀疑……\"，紧扣后文 However, the professors need\nnot worry so much 的转折信号——既然下一句要安抚教授\"不必担心\"，本句必然写他\n们\"心存疑虑\"，doubtful 完美匹配。", "points": ["选词填空", "词性判断·系动词 remain 后接形容词作表语"]}, {"id": "2017-06-1-c-30", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 30, "stem": "", "options": ["A) accurately", "B) confirm", "C) demanding", "D) doubtful", "E) drastically", "F) justify", "G) monopolized", "H) outcome", "I) predominance", "J) presuming", "K) reputation", "L) significant", "M) signify", "N) simultaneously", "O) standardized"], "answer": "H", "analysis": "【答案】H) outcome\n名词\n【词性槽】形容词 learning 后跟名词中心词\n【依据】learning outcome = 学习成果，是教育术语\nlearning outcome 是教育评价里的固定表达，意思是\"学习成果\"，与后文 such as a\ngraduate's ability to investigate and reason 解释\"调查与推理能力\"完全吻合——本段正\n是在说\"用学习成果衡量教学质量\"。\n【竞争词】I) predominance 义不通：学习的优势地位\npredominance 意为\"主导、优势地位\"，learning predominance 在英文里没有这种搭配，也\n不能用\"调查推理能力\"作其例子。\nK) reputation 义不通：学习的名声\nreputation 多与 for/of 搭配表\"某人某事的名声\"，learning reputation 不是地道表达，也与\nsuch as 后\"具体能力\"对应不上。", "points": ["选词填空", "词性判断·形容词 learning 后跟名词中心词"]}, {"id": "2017-06-1-c-31", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 31, "stem": "", "options": ["A) accurately", "B) confirm", "C) demanding", "D) doubtful", "E) drastically", "F) justify", "G) monopolized", "H) outcome", "I) predominance", "J) presuming", "K) reputation", "L) significant", "M) signify", "N) simultaneously", "O) standardized"], "answer": "O", "analysis": "【答案】O) standardized\n形容词\n【词性槽】形容词修饰复数名词 metrics\n【依据】standardized metrics = 标准化的量表\n本段主旨是 Purdue now has a pilot test to assess students' critical thinking skills，要\n客观评估就需要\"标准化的衡量指标\"——standardized 与 metrics/test/measure 是英语里\n高度凝固的搭配，正好回应上文 faculty 对\"教学被量化\"的担忧。\n【竞争词】C) demanding 义偏：苛刻的指标\ndemanding metrics 强调\"要求高、难达标\"，本句重点不在\"难\"而在\"统一、可比\"，与\"科学测\n量三大能力\"主旨不合。\nD) doubtful 义反：可疑的指标\ndoubtful metrics 意为\"令人怀疑的指标\"，与作者\"教授们其实不必担心，因为指标可用\"的肯定\n态度正好相反，逻辑南辕北辙。", "points": ["选词填空", "词性判断·形容词修饰复数名词 metrics"]}, {"id": "2017-06-1-c-32", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 32, "stem": "", "options": ["A) accurately", "B) confirm", "C) demanding", "D) doubtful", "E) drastically", "F) justify", "G) monopolized", "H) outcome", "I) predominance", "J) presuming", "K) reputation", "L) significant", "M) signify", "N) simultaneously", "O) standardized"], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B) confirm\n动词\n【词性槽】并列连词 and 后承接谓语动词\n【依据】本次结果\"印证了\"以往研究\n本段主线是 far fewer students were achieving at high levels on critical thinking than\nthey were doing for written communication or quantitative literacy，新实验得到了和\n以往研究相同的悲观结论——\"confirm earlier studies\"意为\"证实了以往研究的结论\"，与\n开篇 a nationwide study of college graduates had shown that more than a third had\nmade no __(26)__ gains in such mental abilities 前后呼应。\n【竞争词】M) signify 义偏：意味着以往研究\nsignify 表\"意味着、表示\"，宾语通常是抽象事物（significance、importance），signify\nearlier studies 在英语里不成搭配，也不能传达\"印证\"之义。\n【易错】最具迷惑性的错误词是 M) —— signify 与 confirm 中文同译为\"表示\"易混。考生若把\nsignify 当成\"证实\"会与正解 confirm 撞车；防坑：记住 signify 的主语多为符号/现象、宾\n语为抽象意义，而\"实验结果印证以往研究\"的固定搭配只有 confirm earlier studies。", "points": ["选词填空", "词性判断·并列连词 and 后承接谓语动词"]}, {"id": "2017-06-1-c-33", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 33, "stem": "", "options": ["A) accurately", "B) confirm", "C) demanding", "D) doubtful", "E) drastically", "F) justify", "G) monopolized", "H) outcome", "I) predominance", "J) presuming", "K) reputation", "L) significant", "M) signify", "N) simultaneously", "O) standardized"], "answer": "K", "analysis": "【答案】K) reputation\n名词\n【词性槽】形容词 global 修饰的名词\n【依据】reputation for sth = 因……著称\nreputation for + 名词是\"因……享有声誉\"的固定结构，global reputation for excellence\nin teaching 自然意味\"在教学卓越方面享有全球声誉\"，与后文 have only begun to\ndemonstrate what they can produce 形成\"美名在外，但实证不足\"的对比逻辑。\n【竞争词】I) predominance 义偏 + 搭配不成立\npredominance 意为\"主导地位\"，常与 of 搭配（the predominance of X），不与 for 搭，且\n不像 reputation 那样能承接\"as known for excellence\"的语义。", "points": ["选词填空", "词性判断·形容词 global 修饰的名词"]}, {"id": "2017-06-1-c-34", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 34, "stem": "", "options": ["A) accurately", "B) confirm", "C) demanding", "D) doubtful", "E) drastically", "F) justify", "G) monopolized", "H) outcome", "I) predominance", "J) presuming", "K) reputation", "L) significant", "M) signify", "N) simultaneously", "O) standardized"], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C) demanding\n动词\n【词性槽】are 后跟现在分词构成进行时\n【依据】雇主\"要求\"毕业生具备高级思维能力\n本段语境 Knowledge-based degrees are still important, but employers are __(34)__\nadvanced thinking skills from college graduates 转折说\"知识型学位仍重要，但雇主正\n要求……\"，demand sth from sb 是\"向某人要求某物\"的固定搭配，正好对接前文\"批判性思\n维是高校核心目标\"的论点。\n【竞争词】G) monopolized 义不通：雇主垄断思维能力\nmonopolize 意为\"垄断\"，搭配宾语多为 market/industry/conversation；雇主\"垄断毕业生的\n思维能力\"不合逻辑，且形式上 are monopolized 是被动语态，与 advanced thinking skills 作\n宾语冲突。\nJ) presuming 义偏：雇主假定思维能力\npresume 意为\"推测、假定\"，多带 that 从句或抽象事物，are presuming advanced thinking\nskills 表达\"假定其存在\"，与后文 from college graduates 配不上\"向毕业生索取\"的方向。\n【易错】最具迷惑性的错误词是 J) —— presuming 与 demanding 同为 -ing 形式词性正确。考生\n若只看词性会在 demanding/presuming 间犹豫；防坑：盯紧空后的介词 from——\ndemand sth from sb 才是固定搭配，presume sth from sb 不通，看介词比看词义更快。", "points": ["选词填空", "词性判断·are 后跟现在分词构成进行时"]}, {"id": "2017-06-1-c-35", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 35, "stem": "", "options": ["A) accurately", "B) confirm", "C) demanding", "D) doubtful", "E) drastically", "F) justify", "G) monopolized", "H) outcome", "I) predominance", "J) presuming", "K) reputation", "L) significant", "M) signify", "N) simultaneously", "O) standardized"], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A) accurately\n副词\n【词性槽】副词修饰被动语态动词 measured\n【依据】回扣\"准确测量学位价值\"的全文主线\n全文一直在讨论如何客观、可信地证明大学培养出了批判性思维，accurately measured 正\n好抓住这一主线——\"如果大学学位的智识价值能被准确衡量\"，逻辑承接末句 more\npeople will seek higher education—and come out better thinkers。\n【竞争词】N) simultaneously 义不通：同时被衡量\nsimultaneously 强调\"同时发生\"，本句只谈\"衡量学位价值\"一件事，没有并列的另一事件可与之\n同步，搭进去语义悬空。", "points": ["选词填空", "词性判断·副词修饰被动语态动词 measured"]}, {"id": "2017-06-1-m-36", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 36, "stem": "A number of factors are driving down the global oil prices not just for now but in the foreseeable future.", "options": null, "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】多重因素＋未来油价持续走低\n【定位】Oil prices are expected to remain lower for longer. The advent of new technologies has added\nabout 4.2 million barrels per day to the crude oil market, contributing to a global over-supply.\nIn addition, other factors are putting downward pressure on oil prices: change in the strategic\nbehavior of the Organization of Petroleum Exporting Countries, the projected increase in\nIranian exports, the scaling-down of global demand (especially from emerging markets), the\nlong-term drop in petroleum consumption in the United States, and some displacement of oil\nby substitutes.\n预计油价将在更长时间内保持更低。新技术的出现每天给原油市场新增约 420 万桶，导致全\n球供过于求。此外，还有多种因素继续压低油价：欧佩克战略行为的改变、伊朗出口的预期\n增长、全球需求的缩减（尤其是来自新兴市场）、美国石油消费的长期下降，以及替代品对\n石油的部分挤占。\n【改写】A number of factors are driving down the global oil prices ↔\nother factors are putting downward pressure on oil prices\n并列因素＋向下方向的同义替换\nnot just for now but in the foreseeable future ↔\nOil prices are expected to remain lower for longer\n时间长度的同义改写\n【辨邻】 C Oil prices have dropped by over 60% since June 2014 只讲了跌幅事实，没有 A\nnumber of factors 与 foreseeable future 同时落地的并列因素清单。\nOil prices have dropped by over 60% since June 2014\nE Natural gas and coal—also fossil fuels—have similarly seen price declines that\nlook to be long-lived 锁定的是 gas/coal，而非 global oil prices。\nNatural gas and coal—also fossil fuels—have similarly seen price declines that look to be\nlong-lived", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2017-06-1-m-37", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 37, "stem": "Pricing carbon proves the most economical way to reduce greenhouse gas emissions.", "options": null, "answer": "L", "analysis": "【答案】L 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】碳定价＝最经济的减排方式\n【定位】Economic reasoning shows that the least expensive way for each country is to put a price on\ncarbon emissions.\n经济学推理表明，对每个国家而言，最便宜的方式就是给碳排放定价。\n【改写】Pricing carbon ↔ put a price on carbon emissions\n动名词与动词短语的句式转换\nthe most economical way to reduce greenhouse gas emissions ↔\nthe least expensive way for each country is to put a price on carbon emissions\n正反向最高级同义\n【辨邻】 M Setting the right carbon price will therefore efficiently align the costs paid by\ncarbon users with the true social opportunity cost of using carbon 只在论证 价\n格对齐机制，没有把碳定价说成 最便宜的减排路径。\nSetting the right carbon price will therefore efficiently align the costs paid by carbon users with the\ntrue social opportunity cost of using carbon\nN Direct subsidies to research and development have been adopted by some\ngovernments but are a poor substitute for a carbon price 是从反面贬低补贴，没\n有正面给出最高级判断。\nDirect subsidies to research and development have been adopted by some governments but are a\npoor substitute for a carbon price", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2017-06-1-m-38", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 38, "stem": "It is estimated that extreme weather conditions have endangered the lives of millions of African children.", "options": null, "answer": "J", "analysis": "【答案】J 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】极端天气危及数百万非洲儿童\n【定位】For example, the United Nations Children's Fund estimates that some 11 million children in\nAfrica face hunger, disease, and water shortages as a result of the strongest El Nino weather\nphenomenon in decades.\n例如，联合国儿童基金会估计，约 1100 万非洲儿童因数十年来最强的厄尔尼诺天气现象而\n面临饥饿、疾病与缺水。\n【改写】extreme weather conditions ↔ the strongest El Nino weather phenomenon in decades\n具体气候事件 → 上位概括\nmillions of African children ↔ 11 million children in Africa\n具体数字 → 量级上位\nendangered the lives of ↔ face hunger, disease, and water shortages\n结果聚合的同义", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2017-06-1-m-39", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 39, "stem": "The prices of coal are low as a result of over-supply and decreasing demand.", "options": null, "answer": "E", "analysis": "【答案】E 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】煤价因供过于求与需求下降而低\n【定位】Coal prices also are low, owing to over-supply and the scaling-down of demand, especially\nfrom China, which burns half of the world's coal.\n煤炭价格同样很低，其原因是供过于求与需求下降，尤其是来自中国的需求下降，中国消耗\n了全世界一半的煤炭。\n【改写】The prices of coal are low ↔ Coal prices also are low\n主语换序＋同义\nover-supply and decreasing demand ↔ over-supply and the scaling-down of demand\n近义短语逐字对位\n【辨邻】 D contributing to a global over-supply 与 the scaling-down of global demand\n(especially from emerging markets) 都讲油，没讲煤。\ncontributing to a global over-supply", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2017-06-1-m-40", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 40, "stem": "Higher fossil fuel prices prove to be conducive to innovation and application of cleaner technology.", "options": null, "answer": "H", "analysis": "【答案】H 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】高化石燃料价格利于清洁技术创新与应用\n【定位】There is strong evidence that both innovation and adoption of cleaner technology are strongly\nencouraged by higher fossil fuel prices.\n有强力证据表明，更高的化石燃料价格能够有力地推动清洁技术的创新与采用。\n【改写】Higher fossil fuel prices ↔ higher fossil fuel prices\n几乎逐字一致\nconducive to innovation and application of cleaner technology ↔\nboth innovation and adoption of cleaner technology are strongly encouraged\n近义动词＋被动主动转换\n【辨邻】 A low fossil fuel prices could discourage further innovation in, and adoption of,\ncleaner energy technologies 的因（low prices）与果（discourage）都与陈述句\n相反。\nlow fossil fuel prices could discourage further innovation in, and adoption of, cleaner\nenergy technologies", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2017-06-1-m-41", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 41, "stem": "If fossil fuel prices remain low for a long time, it may lead to higher emissions of greenhouse gases.", "options": null, "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】化石燃料长期低价 → 温室气体排放升高\n【定位】Notwithstanding important recent progress in developing renewable fuel sources,\nlow fossil fuel prices could discourage further innovation in, and adoption of, cleaner\nenergy technologies. The result would be higher emissions of carbon dioxide and other\ngreenhouse gases.\n尽管可再生能源近年来取得了重要进展，但低化石燃料价格可能阻碍清洁能源技术的进一步\n创新与采用。其结果将是二氧化碳和其他温室气体的排放升高。\n【改写】fossil fuel prices remain low for a long time ↔ Fossil fuel prices are likely to stay low for long\n时间长度的同义\nit may lead to higher emissions of greenhouse gases ↔\nThe result would be higher emissions of carbon dioxide and other greenhouse gases\n因果连接词的同义＋上位概括\n【辨邻】 H both innovation and adoption of cleaner technology are strongly encouraged\nby higher fossil fuel prices 只立论于 higher prices，没有把 低价持续 → 高排放 这\n条结论说出。\nboth innovation and adoption of cleaner technology are strongly encouraged by higher fossil\nfuel prices\nI The current low fossil fuel price environment will thus certainly delay the\nenergy transition from fossil fuel to clean energy sources 的结论是 延迟转型，与\n陈述句的 排放升高 不对位。\nThe current low fossil fuel price environment will thus certainly delay the energy transition from\nfossil fuel to clean energy sources", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2017-06-1-m-42", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 42, "stem": "Fossil fuels remain the major source of primary energy consumption in today's world.", "options": null, "answer": "G", "analysis": "【答案】G 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】化石燃料仍是一次能源消费主源\n【定位】Renewables account for only a small share of global primary energy consumption, which is\nstill dominated by fossil fuels—30% each for coal and oil, 25% for natural gas.\n可再生能源仅占全球一次能源消费的小部分，化石燃料仍然主导整个消费结构——煤炭与石\n油各占 30%，天然气占 25%。\n【改写】Fossil fuels remain the major source of primary energy consumption ↔\nRenewables account for only a small share of global primary energy consumption, which is\nstill dominated by fossil fuels\n支配关系同义＋正反语义互补\nin today's world ↔ 30% each for coal and oil, 25% for natural gas\n当下数据佐证 现今 这一时间限定\n【辨邻】 E Natural gas and coal—also fossil fuels—have similarly seen price declines that\nlook to be long-lived 关注 价格走低，没说 化石燃料占一次能源消费多少比例。\nNatural gas and coal—also fossil fuels—have similarly seen price declines that look to be\nlong-lived", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2017-06-1-m-43", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 43, "stem": "Even major fossil exporting countries have great potential to develop renewable energies.", "options": null, "answer": "F", "analysis": "【答案】F 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】化石燃料出口大国也有可再生潜力\n【定位】Even Africa and the Middle East, home to economies that are heavily dependent on fossil fuel\nexports, have enormous potential to develop renewables.\n甚至非洲与中东这些严重依赖化石燃料出口的经济体所在地区，也拥有发展可再生能源的巨\n大潜力。\n【改写】Even major fossil exporting countries ↔\nEven Africa and the Middle East, home to economies that are heavily dependent on fossil fuel\nexports\n让步词同义＋具体地区上位\nhave great potential to develop renewable energies ↔\nhave enormous potential to develop renewables\n形容词近义＋同词对位\n【辨邻】 B Policymakers should not allow low energy prices to derail the clean energy\ntransition 焦点在 政策，没讲 fossil exporting countries 的 renewable potential。\nPolicymakers should not allow low energy prices to derail the clean energy transition", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2017-06-1-m-44", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 44, "stem": "Greenhouse gas emissions, if not properly dealt with, will pose endless risks for mankind.", "options": null, "answer": "O", "analysis": "【答案】O 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】温室气体未妥善处理 → 人类无尽风险\n【定位】Failure to address comprehensively the problem of greenhouse gas emissions, however,\nexposes all generations, present and future, to incalculable risks.\n然而，若不能全面应对温室气体排放问题，将让现在与未来的所有世代都面临不可估量的\n风险。\n【改写】if not properly dealt with ↔\nFailure to address comprehensively the problem of greenhouse gas emissions\n条件结构与名词化条件互译\nendless risks for mankind ↔ exposes all generations, present and future, to incalculable risks\n无尽 与 不可估量／所有世代 的同义\n【辨邻】 I Unless renewables become cheap enough that substantial carbon deposits are\nleft underground for a very long time, if not forever, the planet will likely be\nexposed to potentially catastrophic climate risks 的条件是 可再生不够便宜，与陈\n述句 温室气体未妥善处理 的条件不直接对位。\nUnless renewables become cheap enough that substantial carbon deposits are left underground\nfor a very long time, if not forever, the planet will likely be exposed to potentially catastrophic\nclimate risks\nB irreversible and potentially devastating effects of climate change 出现在 政策呼\n吁 的语境里，没有 暴露所有世代于不可估量的风险 这一全人类范围断言。\nirreversible and potentially devastating effects of climate change", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2017-06-1-m-45", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 45, "stem": "It is urgent for governments to increase the cost of using fossil fuels to an appropriate level to lessen the catastrophic effects of climate change.", "options": null, "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B 段（弱锚点）\n【锚点】政府须紧迫提高化石燃料使用成本以减轻气候灾难\n【定位】Action to restore appropriate price incentives, notably through corrective carbon pricing,\nis urgently needed to lower the risk of irreversible and potentially devastating effects of\nclimate change.\n亟需采取行动恢复合适的价格激励，尤其要通过纠正性的碳定价来降低气候变化不可逆且可\n能造成毁灭性影响的风险。\n【改写】It is urgent for governments ↔ is urgently needed\n紧迫性程度词同义\nincrease the cost of using fossil fuels to an appropriate level ↔\nAction to restore appropriate price incentives, notably through corrective carbon pricing\n动作目标的同义概括\nlessen the catastrophic effects of climate change ↔\nlower the risk of irreversible and potentially devastating effects of climate change\n动词同义＋形容词同义\n【辨邻】 L Economic reasoning shows that the least expensive way for each country is to\nput a price on carbon emissions 在论证 最便宜的减排路径，没有 urgent /\nurgently needed 这层紧迫呼吁。\nEconomic reasoning shows that the least expensive way for each country is to put a price on\ncarbon emissions\nM Setting the right carbon price will therefore efficiently align the costs paid by\ncarbon users with the true social opportunity cost of using carbon 阐述机制对\n齐，未喊出 紧迫呼吁 与 减轻气候灾难 的成对结论。\nSetting the right carbon price will therefore efficiently align the costs paid by carbon users with the\ntrue social opportunity cost of using carbon", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2017-06-1-r-46", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 46, "stem": "What do many researchers generally accept?", "options": ["A) It is imperative to protect scientists' patents.", "B) Repositories are essential to scientific research.", "C) Open data sharing is most important to medical science.", "D) Open data sharing is conducive to scientific advancement."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题）\n【判型】What do many researchers generally accept? 是直接问事实层面的群体共识，答案应在\n原文一句话里有明确对应，不需推断。\n【拆句】题干 What do many researchers generally accept? 是简单的 What 引导特殊疑问句，主\n语 many researchers，动词 generally accept。副词 generally 修饰 accept，强调\"普遍\n地\"接受，不是\"全员一致\"，注意范围词。\n【定位】Although many researchers broadly agree that public access to raw data would accelerate\nscience, most are reluctant to post the results of their own labors online.\n尽管许多研究者广泛认同公众获取原始数据将会加速科学发展，但他们大多不愿意把自己劳\n动成果的成品发布到网上。\n【选项】A) 无中生有\n原文从未提及 patents/专利保护这一话题，It is imperative to protect scientists'\npatents 与开放数据共享议题不在同一维度。\nB) 偷换概念\n原文提到 GenBank repository 只是举例说明少数学科已建数据库，并未说\"数据库对科\n学研究是必要的\"；选项 Repositories are essential to scientific research 把举例提升\n为普遍命题。\nC) 以偏概全\n原文谈的是\"开放数据共享对科学（整体）有利\"，并未单独强调 medical science 最\n重要；Open data sharing is most important to medical science 把范围非法缩到\n医学。\nD) ✓ 正确\npublic access to raw data would accelerate science ↔\nOpen data sharing is conducive to scientific advancement\n预判\"开放数据有益于科学\"与 Open data sharing is conducive to scientific\nadvancement 字面同义，无第二项更接近的选项。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2017-06-1-r-47", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 47, "stem": "What is the attitude of most researchers towards making their own data public?", "options": ["A) Opposed.", "B) Ambiguous.", "C) Liberal.", "D) Neutral."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （态度题）\n【判型】What is the attitude of most researchers towards making their own data public? 题\n干含 attitude 一词，且选项均为态度色彩词，按态度题识别。\n【拆句】题干主干 the attitude is what；介词短语 of most researchers 限定\"谁\"的态度，towards\nmaking their own data public 限定态度的对象，动名词 making 作介词宾语。注意 most\n与 their own 两处限定词，不可放大或缩小范围。\n【定位】Although many researchers broadly agree that public access to raw data would accelerate\nscience, most are reluctant to post the results of their own labors online.\n尽管许多研究者广泛认同公开原始数据能加速科学，但他们中的大多数不愿意把自己劳动成\n果的产物发到网上。\n【选项】A) ✓ 正确\nmost are reluctant to post the results of their own labors online ↔ Opposed\n选项中只有 Opposed 是负向词，其余三项均为非负向（中立/含糊/开明），与定位\n句\"reluctant\"极性一致的只有 A。\nB) 偷换概念\nAmbiguous\"含糊的\"暗示态度未明，与原文 reluctant\"明确不愿\"的表态不符。\nC) 与原文相反\nLiberal\"开放/宽容的\"是正向态度，恰与 reluctant 相反，明显与原文矛盾。\nD) 偷换概念\nNeutral\"中立的\"暗示无倾向；但 reluctant 已带明确抗拒，原文没有\"既不赞成也不反\n对\"的余地。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "态度题"]}, {"id": "2017-06-1-r-48", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 48, "stem": "According to the passage, what might hinder open data sharing?", "options": ["A) The fear of massive copying.", "B) The lack of a research culture.", "C) The belief that research data is private intellectual property.", "D) The concern that certain agencies may make a profit out of it."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题）\n【判型】According to the passage, what might hinder open data sharing? 含 According to\nthe passage，直问事实细节，答案应能在原文找到直接对应。\n【拆句】题干主干 what might hinder open data sharing。情态动词 might 表示\"可能\"，意味着\n原文给的是潜在/历史性的阻碍因素而非必然，选项时不要因\"might\"就接受推测性强的项。\n【定位】Historically, scientists have objected to sharing for many reasons: it is a lot of work; until\nrecently, good databases did not exist; grant funders were not pushing for sharing; it has been\ndifficult to agree on standards for formatting data; and there is no agreed way to assign credit\nfor data.\n历史上，科学家因多种原因反对共享：工作量大；好的数据库直到最近才出现；资助方未推\n动共享；难以就数据格式标准达成一致；以及尚未有公认的方式为数据贡献归属功劳。\n【选项】A) 无中生有\n原文阻碍清单中无\"害怕被大量复制\"一项，The fear of massive copying 是凭常识硬塞\n的概念。\nB) 与原文相反\n原文 shift away from a research culture where data is viewed as a private\npreserve 是说要\"摆脱将数据视为私有的研究文化\"——可见原文阻碍是\"私产文化的存\n在\"，而非\"研究文化的缺失\"；The lack of a research culture 颠倒了原意。\nC) ✓ 正确\nthere is no agreed way to assign credit for data ↔\nThe belief that research data is private intellectual property\nshift away from a research culture where data is viewed as a private preserve ↔\nThe belief that research data is private intellectual property\n原文虽用具体说\"无记功方式\"，但其根因即\"把数据当私产\"；第三段 private preserve\n直接给出抽象化表达，与 research data is private intellectual property 字面同义，锁\n定 C。\nD) 无中生有\n原文未提任何\"机构借此牟利\"的担忧；The concern that certain agencies may make\na profit out of it 把现实里其他议题里的常见忧虑硬贴到本文。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2017-06-1-r-49", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 49, "stem": "What helps lift some of the barriers to open data sharing?", "options": ["A) The ever-growing demand for big data.", "B) The advancement of digital technology.", "C) The changing attitude of journals and funders.", "D) The trend of social and economic development."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题）\n【判型】What helps lift some of the barriers to open data sharing? 是问具体原因，答案应在原\n文给出的明确动因句中。\n【拆句】题干主干 What helps lift ...。不定式 to lift 作 helps 的宾语；介词短语 to open data\nsharing 修饰 barriers，限定\"哪类壁垒\"，不要被介词 to 误读为方向。\n【定位】But the barriers are disappearing, in part because journals and funding agencies worldwide\nare encouraging scientists to make their data public.\n但壁垒正在消失，部分原因是全球范围内的期刊与资助机构正在鼓励科学家公开数据。\n【选项】A) 无中生有\n原文从未提及\"对大数据日益增长的需求\"是壁垒消失的原因；The ever-growing\ndemand for big data 借\"大数据\"概念制造时代感，但原文没有这一表述。\nB) 偷换概念\n原文 data can now be shared digitally in ways that were not possible before 提到\n的是\"如今可以数字化共享数据\"，强调\"共享方式\"的可行性而非\"数字技术本身的进步\"；\nThe advancement of digital technology 把\"共享方式\"换成了\"技术本身\"。\nC) ✓ 正确\njournals and funding agencies worldwide are encouraging scientists to make their data public ↔\nThe changing attitude of journals and funders\n预判\"期刊+资助方的态度转变\"与 The changing attitude of journals and funders 字\n面对接，无其他选项达到这种对齐程度。\nD) 无中生有\n原文未提及\"社会经济发展趋势\"作为壁垒消失的原因；The trend of social and\neconomic development 完全是宏观背景词的硬贴。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2017-06-1-r-50", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 50, "stem": "Dryad serves as an example to show how open data sharing ________.", "options": ["A) Is becoming increasingly popular", "B) Benefits sharers and users alike", "C) Makes researchers successful", "D) Saves both money and labor"], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （例证目的题）\n【判型】Dryad serves as an example to show how open data sharing ________. 明确问\"以\nDryad 为例是为了说明\"，例证目的题特征清晰。\n【拆句】题干主干 Dryad serves as an example。不定式 to show how ... 表示\"用来说明\"，how\n引导宾语从句，从句中开放数据共享是主语，谓语被横线挖去，选项是从句的谓语+宾语；\n理解时把题干补全为\"Dryad 这个例子说明开放数据共享会怎样\"。\n【定位】Researchers who share get plenty of personal benefits, including more connections with\ncolleagues, improved visibility and increased citations. The most successful sharers—those\nwhose data are downloaded and cited the most often—get noticed, and their work gets used.\nFor example, one of the most popular data sets on multidisciplinary repository Dryad is about\nwood density around the world; it has been downloaded 5,700 times. Co-author Amy Zanne\nthinks that users probably range from climate-change researchers wanting to estimate how\nmuch carbon is stored in biomass, to foresters looking for information on different grades\nof timber.\n分享数据的研究者能获得大量个人收益，包括与同行更多的联系、更高的能见度和更多的引\n用。最成功的分享者——数据被下载和引用最频繁的那些人——能被看见，其工作得以被使\n用。例如，多学科数据库 Dryad 上最受欢迎的数据集之一是关于全球木材密度的，已被下\n载 5700 次。合作者 Amy Zanne 认为，使用者可能从想估算生物质中储存了多少碳的气候\n变化研究者，到寻找不同等级木材信息的林业人员，应有尽有。\n【选项】A) 偷换概念\n原文 one of the most popular data sets 只是说 Dryad 上某一数据集很受欢迎，并非\n论证\"开放数据共享越来越流行\"这一趋势；is becoming increasingly popular 偏离了\n第四段的论点。\nB) ✓ 正确\nResearchers who share get plenty of personal benefits ↔ benefits sharers and users alike\nusers probably range from climate-change researchers wanting to estimate how much carbon is\nstored in biomass, to foresters looking for information on different grades of timber\n↔ benefits sharers and users alike\n预判\"双方共赢\"既覆盖\"分享者收益\"也覆盖\"使用者从数据中获取所需\"，选项 benefits\nsharers and users alike 的 sharers and users alike 是对该双向获益的精确名词化。\nC) 以偏概全\n原文只提到 The most successful sharers 才能 get noticed，并未说\"开放数据共享让\n所有研究者成功\"；makes researchers successful 把局部条件性的描述拉成普遍因果。\nD) 无中生有\n原文未涉及\"省钱、省人力\"的话题；saves both money and labor 凭一般常识对开放数\n据的好处做臆想，无原文支撑。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "例证目的题"]}, {"id": "2017-06-1-r-51", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 51, "stem": "What does the author say about the shrinking spending of international tourists in the U.S.?", "options": ["A) It is attributable to the rising value of the U.S. dollar.", "B) It is a direct result of the global economic recession.", "C) It reflects a shift of their interest in consumer goods.", "D) It poses a potential threat to the retail business in the U.S."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题）\n【判型】What does the author say about the shrinking spending of international tourists in\nthe U.S.? 问的是作者就一个具体现象给出了什么说法，落点在文章对该现象的客观陈述，\n不要求推断也不要求归纳全文。\n【拆句】What does the author say about the shrinking spending of international tourists in\nthe U.S.? 主干是 What does the author say，about 后挂一个长名词短语 the shrinking\nspending of international tourists in the U.S.，其中 shrinking 是现在分词作定语修饰\nspending，of international tourists 与 in the U.S. 是两个并列的后置介宾，分别限定\"谁\n的消费\"和\"在哪里的消费\"。结构平铺直叙，无陷阱。\n【定位】Its flagship stores in major U.S. cities depend heavily on international tourist spending, which\nshrank at many retailers due to a strong dollar.\n它（Macy）位于美国主要城市的旗舰店严重依赖国际游客的消费，而这部分消费在许多零\n售商那里都因美元走强而缩水了。\n【选项】A) ✓ 正确\ndue to a strong dollar ↔ attributable to the rising value of the U.S. dollar\n预判要求答案点出\"美元走强\"，attributable to the rising value of the U.S. dollar\n把\"美元走强\"重新表达为\"美元价值上升\"，与定位句因果方向、归因对象完全一致，是定\n位句的标准同义替换。\nB) 无中生有\na direct result of the global economic recession 把原因偷换成\"全球经济衰退\"，但\nP1 自始至终只提到 strong dollar 和消费者把钱花在旅游餐饮上，未提及任何\neconomic recession / global downturn。\nC) 张冠李戴\nreflects a shift of their interest in consumer goods 说的是\"国际游客对消费品的兴趣\n发生了转移\"，但 P1 里发生兴趣转移的是 consumers who are more interested in\nspending on travel or dining out than on new clothes or accessories——上下文里\n这个 consumers 指的是国内消费者的总体描述，与前一句\"美元走强导致国际游客消费\n缩水\"是两件不同的事。选项把\"国际游客\"和\"消费偏好转移\"硬凑到一起。\nD) 答非所问\nposes a potential threat to the retail business in the U.S. 说的是\"对美国零售业构成\n潜在威胁\"——这是\"消费缩水所产生的后果\"，而题干问的是 author 对该现象本身的说\n法（原因/属性），方向完全跑偏；并且原文并未把它定性为 a potential threat。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2017-06-1-r-52", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 52, "stem": "What does Macy's believe about its problems?", "options": ["A) They can be solved with better management.", "B) They cannot be attributed to weather only.", "C) They are not as serious in its online stores.", "D) They call for increased investments."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题）\n【判型】What does Macy's believe about its problems? 问的是公司自己对问题的判断，依据是\n原文里 Macy 直接表态的那一句，无需跨段归纳。\n【拆句】What does Macy's believe about its problems? 标准的 What does X believe about Y\n句式，X = Macy's，Y = its problems，its 回指 Macy's。结构简单，无陷阱。\n【定位】However, it's clear that Macy's believes its troubles run deeper than a temporary aberration(偏\n离) off the thermometer.\n然而，显然 Macy 自己也认为，公司面临的麻烦远不止气温偏离这种一次性扰动这么简单。\n【选项】A) 无中生有\ncan be solved with better management 提供了\"通过改善管理可以解决\"这一解决方\n案视角，但 P3 只说公司开始裁员、削减成本，没有任何\"better management 是解\n药\"的判断；公司自己的态度也未把根因归到管理上。\nB) ✓ 正确\nits troubles run deeper than a temporary aberration(偏离) off the thermometer ↔\nThey cannot be attributed to weather only\n预判要求答案表达\"不仅仅是天气\"，They cannot be attributed to weather only 把\nrun deeper than a temporary aberration off the thermometer 的比较关系直接转写\n为 cannot … only 的否定结构，逻辑等值，且 they 准确回指题干的 its problems。\nC) 张冠李戴\nThey are not as serious in its online stores 说\"问题在线上店没那么严重\"——线上线\n下对比确实存在于 One relative bright spot for Macy's during the holiday season\nwas the online channel，但那是文章对节日季销售情况的客观陈述，不是 Macy 关于\nits problems 的判断；选项把题干问\"Macy 怎么看待自己的问题\"偷换为\"问题在哪个渠\n道不严重\"。\nD) 与原文相反\nThey call for increased investments 说\"问题需要加大投资\"，但 $400 million in\ncost-cutting measures 显示公司选择的是削减 4 亿美元开支、裁员关店，方向\n与\"increased investments\"恰好相反。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2017-06-1-r-53", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 53, "stem": "In order to cut costs, Macy's decided to ________.", "options": ["A) Cut the salary of senior executives", "B) Relocate some of its chain stores", "C) Adjust its promotion strategies", "D) Reduce the size of its staff"], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题）\n【判型】In order to cut costs, Macy's decided to ________. 是个待补全的陈述句，要求从原文里找\n出公司为削减成本作出的具体决定，落点在动作清单层面的事实。\n【拆句】In order to cut costs, Macy's decided to ________. 主干是 Macy's decided to + 动词原\n形，In order to cut costs 是不定式作目的状语，置于句首。需要补全的是 decided to 后\n面的不定式动词短语，选项必须是动词原形开头的动宾短语。结构无陷阱。\n【定位】The company pledged to cut 600 back-office positions, though some 150 workers in those\nroles would be reassigned to other jobs. It also plans to offer \"voluntary separation\" packages\nto 165 senior executives. It will slash staffing at its fleet of 770 stores, a move affecting some\n3,000 employees.\n公司承诺裁掉 600 个后台岗位，尽管其中约 150 名员工会被重新安置到其他岗位；公司还\n计划向 165 名高管提供\"自愿离职\"补偿方案；并将削减旗下 770 家门店的人员配置，此举\n将影响约 3000 名员工。\n【选项】A) 偷换概念\ncut the salary of senior executives 把\"砍高管工资\"安到 Macy 头上，但 offer\n\"voluntary separation\" packages to 165 senior executives 说的是\"向 165 名高管提\n供自愿离职补偿\"——是让人离开公司而不是降工资，动作对象和性质都不同。\nB) 偷换概念\nrelocate some of its chain stores 说\"搬迁部分连锁店\"，但 announced the locations\nof 36 stores it will close in early 2016 明确是 close（关闭）而不是 relocate（搬迁）\n——同时关店属于 P4 而非 53 题所在的成本削减清单段。\nC) 无中生有\nadjust its promotion strategies 是商业语境下听起来很合理的动作，但 P3 全段没有任\n何关于 promotion / marketing / discount 等促销层面的调整，5.2% 销售下滑那段也\n未提及。\nD) ✓ 正确\nslash staffing at its fleet of 770 stores, a move affecting some 3,000 employees ↔\nreduce the size of its staff\ncut 600 back-office positions ↔ reduce the size of its staff\n预判要求答案概括为\"减少员工\"，reduce the size of its staff 用最稳的上位动词\nreduce + 名词短语 the size of its staff 同时覆盖后台裁员、高管离职、店员削减三条\n动作，是定位句群的标准上位转述。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2017-06-1-r-54", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 54, "stem": "Why does Macy's plan to expand Bluemercury in 2016?", "options": ["A) To experiment on its new business concept.", "B) To focus more on beauty products than clothing.", "C) To promote sales of its products by lowering prices.", "D) To be more competitive in sales of beauty products."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题）\n【判型】Why does Macy's plan to expand Bluemercury in 2016? 问的是 Macy 扩张\nBluemercury 这一动作背后的原因，文章里通常会用 hope / to / so that 等方式直接陈述\n目的，无需推断。\n【拆句】Why does Macy's plan to expand Bluemercury in 2016? 主干是 Why does Macy's\nplan to expand Bluemercury，in 2016 是时间状语。需要注意 plan to expand 是\"打算\n扩张\"，不是\"扩张本身\"——回原文找的是这个计划背后的动机。结构平铺直叙，无陷阱。\n【定位】At a time when young beauty shoppers are often turning to Sephora or Ulta instead of\ndepartment store beauty counters, Macy's hopes Bluemercury will help strengthen its position\nin the category.\n在年轻美妆消费者越来越多地转向 Sephora 或 Ulta 而非百货美妆专柜的时代，Macy 希望\n借助 Bluemercury 强化自己在美妆品类中的地位。\n【选项】A) 张冠李戴\nexperiment on its new business concept 把扩张 Bluemercury 说成\"试水新业务概\n念\"，但 Bluemercury, the beauty chain it bought last year 指明 Bluemercury 是去\n年就已经收购的成熟连锁，并非新业务概念；真正属于\"新概念\"的是同段提到的 Macy's\nBackstage（a newly-developed off-price concept），与 Bluemercury 不是同一\n对象。\nB) 过度推断\nfocus more on beauty products than clothing 把扩张目的说成\"比起服装更看重美\n妆\"，引入了\"美妆 vs 服装\"的对立，但 P5 全段只比较 Bluemercury 与 Sephora、Ulta\n这些美妆同行，并未把美妆与服装做战略层面的取舍比较。\nC) 张冠李戴\npromote sales of its products by lowering prices 谈降价促销，原文里与\"降价\"挂钩\n的是 a newly-developed off-price concept——而 off-price 是 Macy's Backstage 的\n属性，不是 Bluemercury 的属性；Bluemercury 是高端美妆连锁，原文未提到任何\nlowering prices 的策略。\nD) ✓ 正确\nstrengthen its position in the category ↔ To be more competitive in sales of beauty products\nBluemercury, the beauty chain it bought last year ↔\nTo be more competitive in sales of beauty products\n预判要求答案表达\"在美妆领域更强\"，To be more competitive in sales of beauty\nproducts 用 more competitive 替换 strengthen its position，用 sales of beauty\nproducts 替换 the category，与定位句目的方向一致。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2017-06-1-r-55", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 55, "stem": "What can we learn about Macy's during the holiday season?", "options": ["A) Sales dropped sharply in its physical stores.", "B) Its retail sales exceeded those of T. J. Maxx.", "C) It helped Bluemercury establish its position worldwide.", "D) It filled its stores with abundant supply of merchandise."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题）\n【判型】What can we learn about Macy's during the holiday season? 问的是节日季 Macy 的整\n体情况，虽然措辞是 What can we learn，但选项指向的都是文章已经直接陈述的事实（在\n线增长、实体店暴跌等），不要求跨段归纳，本质是细节题。\n【拆句】What can we learn about Macy's during the holiday season? 主干是 What can we\nlearn about Macy's，during the holiday season 是时间状语限定范围。注意 learn 在这\n里是\"得知、了解到\"的意思，不是\"学习\"。结构简单，无陷阱。\n【定位】While Thanksgiving, Black Friday and Cyber Monday all saw record spending online, in-\nstore sales plunged over the holiday weekend.\n尽管感恩节、黑色星期五和网络星期一三天的线上消费都创下了纪录，但实体店在节日周末\n的销售却暴跌了。\n【选项】A) ✓ 正确\nin-store sales plunged over the holiday weekend ↔\nSales dropped sharply in its physical stores\n预判要求答案命中\"线上涨/实体店跌\"中的一端，Sales dropped sharply in its physical\nstores 精准对应 While 对照句的主句端\"in-store sales plunged\"——in-store 与\nphysical stores、plunged 与 dropped sharply 都是教科书级的同义对应。\nB) 无中生有\nIts retail sales exceeded those of T. J. Maxx 说 Macy 零售销售超过了 T. J. Maxx，但\nP5 里 T. J. Maxx 只是被作为 Macy's Backstage 的竞争对手提及，整文从未做过 Macy\n与 T. J. Maxx 的销售数字对比；尤其 P6 节日季内容里更未提到 T. J. Maxx。\nC) 过度推断\nhelped Bluemercury establish its position worldwide 把\"帮 Bluemercury 在全球确\n立地位\"安到节日季上，但 P5 关于 Bluemercury 的扩张目的是 strengthen its\nposition in the category，并无\"worldwide\"的范围描述，且与节日季也无关。\nD) 偷换概念\nfilled its stores with abundant supply of merchandise 谈\"门店货品供应充足\"，P6\n出现的 filled 是 the number of orders it filled——指线上履行的订单数量，filled 在\n那里是\"完成订单\"，与\"填满门店货架\"完全不同；选项利用 filled 一词的多义性诱导。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}]});
